window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-hfo"] = {"id": "jammu-hfo", "title": "University of Jammu M.A., code HFO-20622-A", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance, booklet code HFO-20622-A, year not printed (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · year not printed · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "In the year 1890 (printed as \"1989\"), which of the following books was published by William James?", "options": {"a": "Basics of Psychology", "b": "Pretext of Psychology", "c": "Principles of Psychology", "d": "Attention & Perception"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: William James published The Principles of Psychology in 1890. William James (1890), often called the father of American psychology, spent about 12 years writing this two-volume work of nearly 1,400 pages, and it laid the groundwork for functionalism, the American school that asks what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Its best-known chapters describe the stream of consciousness (thought is personal, always changing, continuous and selective), habit as \"the enormous fly-wheel of society\", the self split into the knowing \"I\" and the known \"Me\" (material, social and spiritual selves), and the theory of emotion that James shared with Carl Lange (1885). Students found the big book heavy, so James wrote a one-volume abridgement, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892), which they nicknamed \"the Jimmy\". Example: when Meera notices that her thoughts while studying drift from an exam to dinner to a film song without any clear breaks, she is describing what James called the stream of consciousness. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is the title James published in 1890. Exam trap: the fillers borrow real psychology words; attention and perception are chapters inside the Principles, not a separate book, and the title should not be confused with Wilhelm Wundt's Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874). Key note: the paper prints the year as \"1989\", a misprint for 1890; it does not change the answer, so c stands.", "topic": "William James's Principles", "fig": "William James"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the goal of psychology?", "options": {"a": "Description", "b": "Explanation", "c": "Prediction", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: description, explanation and prediction are all goals of psychology. Introductory texts list four goals of psychology as a science: to describe, explain, predict and control (or influence) behaviour and mental processes. Description means observing carefully and recording what happens, such as how often a child hits classmates. Explanation means finding the causes, usually by building and testing a theory: is the hitting copied from television, rewarded by attention, or a sign of frustration? Prediction means using that knowledge to forecast when the behaviour will occur again. Control, the fourth goal, means using it to change behaviour for the better, as therapists, teachers and health workers do. Each goal builds on the one before: you cannot explain what you have not described, and a good explanation allows accurate prediction. Basic research mainly serves the first three goals; applied research adds control. Example: Arjun, a school psychologist, notes that Kabir disrupts class mainly during maths (description), links this to fear of failure (explanation), expects trouble before the next maths test (prediction) and plans extra support to prevent it (control). Option d is right because description, explanation and prediction are each recognised goals, so all three listed options qualify. Exam trap: students who remember one headline goal, often prediction or understanding, pick a single option; when every listed item is a true goal, \"All of the above\" is the answer, and the absence of control from the list does not change that.", "topic": "Goals of psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which of the following, the behaviour is controlled by the consequences?", "options": {"a": "Operant Conditioning", "b": "Classical Conditioning", "c": "Consequent Conditioning", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in operant conditioning behaviour is controlled by its consequences. Operant (instrumental) conditioning is learning in which the likelihood of a voluntary behaviour changes according to what follows it. Edward Thorndike (1898) put hungry cats in puzzle boxes; they escaped faster over trials because responses followed by food were \"stamped in\", a principle he later stated as the law of effect (1911). B. F. Skinner (1937, a year before The Behavior of Organisms) coined the word \"operant\" for a response that operates on the environment, and studied rats and pigeons in the operant chamber (the Skinner box). Reinforcement raises the rate of a behaviour: positive reinforcement adds something pleasant, negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant. Punishment lowers the rate, and extinction follows when reinforcement stops. Example: Riya's parents praise her each time she finishes her homework before dinner, and soon she does it every day; the praise, a consequence, controls the behaviour. Option a is right because consequences (reinforcement and punishment) are the defining cause of change in operant learning. Exam trap: classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) is also learning, but there the response is elicited by a stimulus that comes before it; the dog salivates to the bell whether or not anything follows. Remember: classical is about what comes before (stimulus to stimulus), operant about what comes after (response to consequence).", "topic": "Operant conditioning and consequences", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "To measure perceived intensity Stevens used:", "options": {"a": "Magnitude Production", "b": "Magnitude Estimation", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Stanley Smith Stevens measured perceived intensity by magnitude estimation. Psychophysics links the physical strength of a stimulus to the strength of the sensation. Gustav Fechner (1860) had assumed that each just noticeable difference is an equal step of sensation and derived a logarithmic law. Stanley Smith Stevens (1957) asked people directly instead. In magnitude estimation an observer is given a standard (say a light called \"10\") and then assigns every other stimulus a number in proportion to how strong it feels: a light that seems twice as bright gets \"20\". Plotting these numbers against physical intensity gave Stevens' power law, S = kI^n, where the exponent n differs by sense. For brightness n is about 0.33 (compression: a big rise in light feels like a small rise in brightness); for electric shock it is about 3.5 (expansion: a small rise in current feels far stronger). Example: in a lab, Kabir hears a tone called \"10\" and calls a louder one \"30\" because it seems three times as loud; that is magnitude estimation. Option b is right because assigning numbers to felt strength is the method on which Stevens built his power law. Exam trap: magnitude production sounds like a twin, but it reverses the task: the observer is given a number and adjusts the stimulus until it feels that strong. Key note: Stevens also used magnitude production and cross-modality matching as cross-checks, so \"Both\" has a case; standard perception textbooks (for example E. Bruce Goldstein's Sensation and Perception) name magnitude estimation as his method, so b stands.", "topic": "Stevens' magnitude estimation", "fig": "Stanley Smith Stevens"}, {"q": "Haptic memory involves:", "options": {"a": "Cutaneous Subsystem", "b": "Kinesthetic Subsystem", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: haptic memory involves both the cutaneous and the kinaesthetic subsystems. Haptic memory is the brief sensory store for touch, the touch counterpart of iconic memory for vision and echoic memory for sound, the two stores Ulric Neisser (1967) named. George Sperling's (1960) partial-report studies showed how much the brief visual store holds; touch has been studied far less. Everything turns on what \"haptic\" means. Jack Loomis and Susan Lederman (1986) divided touch into the cutaneous subsystem, receptors in the skin that signal pressure, vibration, texture and temperature, and the kinaesthetic subsystem, receptors in muscles, tendons and joints that signal limb position and movement. Perception through the skin alone, with the hand still, is tactile perception; haptic perception uses both subsystems together, as when you actively explore an object with moving fingers. Haptic memory therefore briefly holds information from both channels after contact. Example: Meera reaches into her bag without looking and finds her keys by their cold metal edges (cutaneous) and by the shape her fingers trace as they move round them (kinaesthetic); both kinds of information are held for a moment as she recognises the bunch. Option c is right because haptic information, and so haptic memory, combines skin and movement signals. Exam trap: students equate \"haptic\" with skin touch alone and pick a, or with body movement alone and pick b; haptic means the active combination of both.", "topic": "Haptic memory subsystems", "fig": ""}, {"q": "As per which rule does the cellular basis of learning involve strengthening of a synapse?", "options": {"a": "Hebb's Rule", "b": "Sherrington's Rule", "c": "James's Rule", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: Hebb's rule says the cellular basis of learning is the strengthening of a synapse. Donald Hebb (1949), in The Organization of Behavior, proposed how experience could change the brain. His postulate: when an axon of cell A is near enough to excite cell B and repeatedly or persistently takes part in firing it, some growth process or metabolic change occurs so that A's efficiency in firing B increases. In plain words, the connection between neurons that are active together grows stronger; a later paraphrase, often credited to Carla Shatz (1992), is \"cells that fire together wire together\". Groups of neurons linked in this way form cell assemblies that hold a memory. Physiological support came with long-term potentiation (LTP): Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) stimulated a pathway into the rabbit hippocampus at high frequency and found that the synaptic response stayed larger for hours afterwards. The NMDA receptor, which opens only when the sending and receiving cells are active together, is Hebb's rule in molecular form. Example: each time Arjun hears the word \"chai\" while smelling it, the neurons for the word and the smell fire together and their link strengthens, until the word alone brings the smell to mind. Option a is right because the rule that ties learning to synaptic strengthening is Hebb's. Exam trap: Charles Sherrington (1897) coined the word \"synapse\", so his name tempts students, but he studied reflexes and the integrative action of the nervous system, not a rule of learning.", "topic": "Hebb's rule of synaptic learning", "fig": "Donald Hebb"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the part of hippocampus?", "options": {"a": "Field CA3", "b": "Field C3A", "c": "Field AC3", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: field CA3 is a part of the hippocampus. The hippocampus proper is also called the cornu ammonis (Ammon's horn), because in cross-section it curls like a ram's horn; CA stands for cornu ammonis. Rafael Lorente de No (1934) divided it into fields CA1, CA2, CA3 and CA4, the last now usually treated as the hilus of the dentate gyrus. Information flows through the trisynaptic circuit: from the entorhinal cortex along the perforant path to the dentate gyrus, from the dentate gyrus along the mossy fibres to CA3, and from CA3 along the Schaffer collaterals to CA1, which sends output back towards the cortex. CA3 is special because its cells have rich recurrent connections with one another, which is thought to let a partial cue reactivate a whole stored pattern (pattern completion). CA1 is very sensitive to loss of oxygen: patient R.B., described by Stuart Zola-Morgan, Larry Squire and David Amaral (1986), lost CA1 cells after an interruption of blood supply to the brain and became amnesic. Example: when Riya smells jasmine and the whole scene of her cousin's wedding comes back, CA3's pattern completion is thought to play a part. Option a is right because CA3 is a named field of Ammon's horn. Exam trap: the distractors scramble the same letters (C3A, AC3); the correct form is always CA followed by a number from 1 to 4.", "topic": "Hippocampal fields (CA3)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The process by which short term memories are converted into long term memories is called:", "options": {"a": "Elongation", "b": "Consolidation", "c": "Potentiation", "d": "Both (A) & (B)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: consolidation is the process by which short-term memories are converted into long-term memories. A new memory is fragile at first and easily disrupted; consolidation makes it stable. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) named the process after finding that learning a second list soon after the first disrupted recall of the first, as if the first trace had not yet set. Two levels are now distinguished. Synaptic (cellular) consolidation takes minutes to hours and needs new protein synthesis at the synapse. Systems consolidation takes weeks to years, as the hippocampus gradually hands the memory over to the cortex. Evidence: a head injury or electroconvulsive therapy disrupts the most recent memories most, while older memories survive (Theodule Ribot's law, 1881: the new perishes before the old). Patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), whose medial temporal lobes were removed by the surgeon William Scoville in 1953, could no longer form new long-term declarative memories (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), though most older memories were spared. Sleep after learning also aids consolidation. Example: Kabir revises his notes the night before an exam; if he sleeps well, more of them are consolidated and still there in the morning. Option b is right because consolidation is the technical name for this conversion. Exam trap: potentiation, as in long-term potentiation (LTP), is a cellular mechanism that may underlie consolidation, not the name of the process itself.", "topic": "Memory consolidation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of self-actualization?", "options": {"a": "Maslow", "b": "Rogers", "c": "Franklin", "d": "Allport"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Abraham Maslow gave the concept of self-actualisation. Abraham Maslow (1943), in \"A Theory of Human Motivation\" and later in Motivation and Personality (1954), arranged needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming (\"What a man can be, he must be\"). The lower deficiency needs must be reasonably met before the growth need of self-actualisation drives behaviour. Maslow studied people he judged to be self-actualisers, such as Abraham Lincoln, Thomas Jefferson, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described them as accepting of self and others, spontaneous, problem-centred, independent and prone to peak experiences. Example: Riya has a secure job, a loving family and respect at work, yet in her thirties she starts learning Hindustani classical music simply to grow as a person; Maslow would call this self-actualisation. Option a is right because textbooks credit self-actualisation, as the peak of the need hierarchy, to Maslow. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1951) also wrote about an actualising tendency, so examiners hope you will pick b. Key note: Kurt Goldstein (1939, The Organism) coined the term, and Carl Rogers built his theory on an actualising tendency, but textbooks credit the concept as a need-hierarchy idea to Maslow, so a stands.", "topic": "Maslow and self-actualisation", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "A culturally determined rule that modifies expression of emotion in a particular situation is called as:", "options": {"a": "Cultural Rule", "b": "Display Rule", "c": "Situational Rule", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: a culturally determined rule that modifies the expression of emotion in a particular situation is a display rule. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1969) coined the term. Their research suggested that a few basic facial expressions (happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust) are universal, so cultures differ mainly in rules about when, where and to whom these expressions may be shown. A display rule can tell a person to intensify, weaken, neutralise or mask a feeling with another expression. In the classic study, Wallace Friesen (1972) filmed Japanese and American students watching stressful film clips. Alone, both groups showed the same expressions of disgust and fear; when an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students more often masked negative expressions with smiles. Display rules are learned in childhood; by school age children know to hide disappointment over an unwanted gift. Example: at her grandmother's funeral Meera wants to laugh at a cousin's joke but keeps a solemn face, and at a wedding she smiles although she is exhausted; both are display rules at work. Option b is right because display rule is the technical name for a cultural norm that changes how emotion is shown. Exam trap: feeling rules (Arlie Hochschild, 1979) sound similar but concern what one should feel, not what one may show; and \"cultural rule\" is a vague phrase, not the term.", "topic": "Display rules of emotion", "fig": "Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen"}, {"q": "To test James-Lange's theory Hohmann collected data from the persons with:", "options": {"a": "Brain Damage", "b": "Spinal Cord Damage", "c": "Occipital Damage", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: George Hohmann tested the James-Lange theory with people who had spinal cord damage. The James-Lange theory, proposed by William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), says that an emotion is our perception of bodily changes: we feel afraid because we tremble. If that is true, people who receive less feedback from the body should feel weaker emotions. George Hohmann (1966) interviewed 25 male veterans with spinal cord injuries at different levels. The higher the lesion on the spinal cord, the less bodily feedback reaches the brain, and the more the men reported that their fear, anger and sexual feelings had lost intensity; one said his anger \"just doesn't have the heat to it that it used to\". This supported James and Lange. Example: Arjun, injured high in the neck in a road accident, says he still knows when something is unfair but no longer feels the surge of anger in his chest. Option b is right because Hohmann's participants were people with spinal cord damage. Exam trap: Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the same theory with animals, citing Charles Sherrington's dogs whose spinal cords had been cut and his own cats whose sympathetic nerves had been removed, which still showed emotional behaviour; students mix up the two lines of evidence. Note too that a later study by Kathleen Chwalisz, Ed Diener and Dennis Gallagher (1988) found people with spinal cord injuries reporting emotions about as intense as anyone else's.", "topic": "Hohmann's spinal cord study", "fig": "George Hohmann"}, {"q": "The study of reaction time is called as:", "options": {"a": "Mental Chronometry", "b": "Mental Chronology", "c": "Reaction Time Studies", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the study of reaction time as a window on mental processes is called mental chronometry. Mental chronometry measures how long mental operations take, using reaction time (RT), the interval between a stimulus and the response to it. It rests on the idea that mental processes take time and that each extra stage adds measurable time. Franciscus Donders (1868) founded it with the subtraction method: he subtracted simple RT (respond as soon as any light appears) from the RT of a task with an added stage, such as identifying the stimulus or choosing between responses, and treated the difference as the time that stage needs. Saul Sternberg (1966) timed memory scanning, showing that RT rose by about 38 milliseconds for each extra item held in memory, and later (1969) proposed the additive factors method. Michael Posner (1978) gathered such work in Chronometric Explorations of Mind. Example: in a lab, Meera presses a button as soon as a light flashes, then must press only for red lights and ignore green ones; she is slower the second time, and the extra time estimates how long identifying the colour takes. Option a is right because mental chronometry is the established term for using reaction time to study the mind. Exam trap: \"mental chronology\" looks almost the same, but chronology means the order of past events, not the timing of mental processes; \"reaction time studies\" is a description, not the name of the field.", "topic": "Mental chronometry", "fig": "Franciscus Donders"}, {"q": "A supporting cell found in the organ of Corti is known as:", "options": {"a": "Deiters' Cell", "b": "Hair Cell", "c": "Auditory Cell", "d": "Both (B) & (C)"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Deiters' cell is a supporting cell found in the organ of Corti. The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane inside the cochlea of the inner ear, and it contains two kinds of cells. The receptor cells are the hair cells: about 3,500 inner hair cells in a single row, which send almost all the signals to the auditory nerve, and about 12,000 outer hair cells in three rows, which amplify the vibration. Around them are supporting cells that hold the structure in shape. Deiters' cells, also called outer phalangeal cells and named after the German anatomist Otto Deiters (1834 to 1863), sit below the outer hair cells and cradle their bases, sending thin processes up to the surface between them. Other supporting cells include the pillar cells, which form the tunnel of Corti, and the Hensen's and Claudius cells at the outer edge. When sound moves the basilar membrane, the stereocilia of the hair cells bend against the tectorial membrane, ion channels open and the hair cell signals the nerve; the supporting cells keep the hair cells in the right position for this. Option a is right because Deiters' cells support the outer hair cells rather than detecting sound themselves. Exam trap: hair cells are the best-known cells of the organ of Corti, so students pick b, but hair cells are the sensory receptors that transduce sound, not supporting cells.", "topic": "Supporting cells of Corti", "fig": "Otto Deiters"}, {"q": "Which of the following is involved in pitch perception?", "options": {"a": "Place Code", "b": "Rate Code", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: both the place code and the rate code are involved in pitch perception. Pitch is the perceived highness or lowness of a sound, set mainly by its frequency (humans hear about 20 to 20,000 Hz). The place code comes from the place theory of Hermann von Helmholtz (1863): different frequencies make different places on the basilar membrane vibrate most. Georg von Bekesy confirmed this with his travelling wave (Nobel Prize 1961): high frequencies peak near the narrow, stiff base of the cochlea and low frequencies near the wide, floppy apex, so the brain reads pitch from which hair cells are most active. The rate (temporal or frequency) code comes from the frequency theory of William Rutherford (1886): auditory nerve fibres fire in time with the sound wave, so firing rate signals pitch. A single neuron cannot fire more than about 1,000 times a second, so Ernest Wever and Charles Bray (1930) proposed the volley principle: groups of fibres take turns and together keep pace with higher frequencies. The modern view combines the two: timing (phase locking) carries pitch for low frequencies, up to about 4,000 to 5,000 Hz, and place carries it for high frequencies. Example: when Riya hears a deep tabla stroke, the timing of nerve firing tells her its pitch; for a very high whistle above 5,000 Hz, the place of peak vibration does the work. Option c is right because both codes contribute. Exam trap: choosing only a or b treats the two theories as rivals; and the frequency theory is William Rutherford's, not the physicist Ernest Rutherford's.", "topic": "Place and rate codes of pitch", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A G protein that plays a vital role in transduction of sweetness and bitterness is called:", "options": {"a": "Gustducin", "b": "Amyloid", "c": "Umami", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: gustducin is the G protein that plays a vital role in transducing sweet and bitter tastes. Taste cells work in two broad ways. Salty and sour tastes mainly use ion channels: sodium or hydrogen ions enter the taste cell directly. Sweet, bitter and umami tastes use receptors coupled to G proteins: the taste molecule binds a receptor on the cell surface, the receptor activates a G protein inside the cell, and a chain of second messengers ends in the release of transmitter onto the taste nerve. Susan McLaughlin, Peter McKinnon and Robert Margolskee (1992) discovered this taste-cell G protein and called it gustducin, because its alpha subunit closely resembles transducin, the G protein of the rods and cones of the retina (gust, from taste, plus transducin). Mice bred without gustducin lose much of their sensitivity to bitter and sweet substances, which shows how vital it is. Example: when Arjun sips bitter karela juice, the bitter molecules bind receptors that activate gustducin in his taste cells, the taste nerve fires, and he winces. Option a is right because gustducin is the named G protein of sweet and bitter transduction. Exam trap: umami sits in the same chapter and is also transduced through G protein receptors, but umami is a taste quality (the savoury taste of glutamate, named by Kikunae Ikeda in 1908), not a protein.", "topic": "Gustducin and taste transduction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A step by step procedure involved in figuring out the correct answer to any problem is known as:", "options": {"a": "Algorithm", "b": "Heuristics", "c": "Means End Analysis", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: an algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that leads to the correct answer to a problem. Problem solving means moving from an initial state to a goal state, and psychologists separate two broad kinds of strategy. An algorithm is a fixed set of rules which, if followed exactly, always produces the solution: long division, a recipe followed to the letter, or trying every possible code on a padlock. Its weakness is time; when there are many possibilities an algorithm can be very slow. A heuristic is a rule of thumb that is quick and usually works but gives no guarantee. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon (1972), who modelled human problem solving with their General Problem Solver program, described means-end analysis, a heuristic in which you find the biggest difference between where you are and the goal and set a subgoal to reduce it. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) showed how judgement heuristics such as availability, representativeness and anchoring lead to systematic errors. Example: Kabir has forgotten the three-digit code of his bicycle lock. Trying 000, 001, 002 and so on up to 999 is an algorithm: slow but certain. Starting with the digits of his birth year is a heuristic: fast, but it may fail. Option a is right because only an algorithm is defined as a step-by-step procedure that guarantees the answer. Exam trap: heuristics and means-end analysis are also problem-solving strategies, so \"All of these\" tempts, but both trade the guarantee for speed.", "topic": "Algorithms versus heuristics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The unit of measurement in statistics is:", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mode", "d": "Standard Deviation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the standard deviation is the unit of measurement in statistics. The standard deviation (SD, sigma) is the square root of the average squared deviation of scores from the mean; it tells you the typical distance of a score from the mean, in the original units. Karl Pearson (1894) gave it its name. Because it is a stable, standard distance, statisticians use it as a measuring stick for deviations. A z score expresses any deviation in SD units: z = (X minus mean) / SD, so a z of 2 means two SDs above the mean whatever the test. The normal curve table is also written in SD units: about 68 per cent of cases lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. Example: Riya scores 70 on one test with mean 60 and SD 5, and 70 on another with mean 60 and SD 20. In SD units the first score is 2 SDs above the mean and the second only 0.5, so the first is the more impressive. Option d is right because only the SD works as a unit for measuring distance from the average. Exam trap: mean, median and mode are measures of central tendency; each is a single point in the distribution, the place you measure from, not the unit you measure with. Key note: the stem is loosely worded; it means the unit in which deviations are measured (psychology statistics texts such as Henry Garrett's Statistics in Psychology and Education measure distances along the normal curve in sigma units), and d stands.", "topic": "Standard deviation as unit", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher wants to group people into low, average and high categories, the most suitable method to do this is:", "options": {"a": "Quartile Deviation", "b": "Frequency", "c": "Mean", "d": "Chi-Square Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: quartile deviation is the most suitable of these methods for grouping people into low, average and high categories. Quartiles are the three points that divide a ranked distribution into four equal parts. Q1, the 25th percentile, has a quarter of the scores below it; Q2 is the median; Q3, the 75th percentile, has three quarters below it. People below Q1 can be called low, those between Q1 and Q3 (the middle 50 per cent) average, and those above Q3 high. The quartile deviation, or semi-interquartile range, is built on exactly these points: Q = (Q3 minus Q1) / 2. Because it ignores the extreme quarters, it resists outliers and is the natural partner of the median. Example: a counsellor gives a stress scale to 200 students and finds Q1 is 32 and Q3 is 58. Meera, at 25, falls in the low group; Arjun, at 45, is average; Kabir, at 66, is high. Option a is right because the quartile approach supplies the cut-off points that create the three groups. Exam trap: the mean is a single point, so it can split a group into two (above and below average) but not into three; frequency is only a count of how many people got each score; and chi-square is a test of whether observed frequencies differ from expected ones. Key note: strictly it is the quartile points (Q1 and Q3) that do the grouping, and mean plus or minus 1 SD cut-offs are also used; among the four options only quartile deviation rests on those points, so a stands.", "topic": "Quartiles for grouping scores", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The normal probability curve is not:", "options": {"a": "Asymptotic to the Baseline", "b": "Bell Shaped", "c": "Leptokurtic", "d": "Mesokurtic"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the normal probability curve is not leptokurtic. The normal curve is the symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) as the limit of the binomial distribution and used by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) for errors in astronomical measurement. Its properties: it is bell-shaped and unimodal; it is symmetrical, so the mean, median and mode coincide at the centre; it is asymptotic to the baseline, meaning the tails come ever closer to the x-axis but never touch it; its total area is 1, so areas read as probabilities; and it is fully described by its mean and SD, with about 68, 95 and 99.7 per cent of cases within 1, 2 and 3 SD. Kurtosis describes how peaked a curve is, and how heavy its tails are, compared with the normal. The normal curve is the reference point: mesokurtic, with a moment kurtosis of 3 (excess kurtosis 0). A leptokurtic curve is more peaked with heavier tails (kurtosis above 3); a platykurtic curve is flatter (below 3). Example: Kabir plots the ratings of 300 students on a scale where almost everyone marks close to the average and only a few give extreme answers; that tall, thin curve with long tails is leptokurtic, not normal. Option c is right because the normal curve is mesokurtic by definition, so it cannot be leptokurtic. Exam trap: this is a \"not\" question; a, b and d are all true properties, and examiners count on students skimming past \"not\", or confusing lepto (slender, peaked) with meso (middle).", "topic": "Properties of the normal curve", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "options": {"a": "Managerial significance matters more than statistical significance", "b": "Statistical significance matters more than managerial significance", "c": "Raw score can be converted into z score", "d": "Z scores are standard scores"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the incorrect statement is that statistical significance matters more than managerial significance. Statistical significance tells you only that a result is unlikely to be due to chance alone (p below .05, say). It depends heavily on sample size: with thousands of respondents even a tiny difference becomes significant. Managerial (practical) significance asks whether the difference is big enough to matter for a decision. Business research texts, such as Donald Cooper and Pamela Schindler's Business Research Methods, stress that a statistically significant but trivial result should not drive decisions, so managerial significance matters more. Psychology makes the same point: Jacob Cohen (1994), in \"The earth is round (p < .05)\", attacked blind reliance on p values and urged effect sizes, and Neil Jacobson and Paula Truax (1991) proposed testing clinical significance as reliable change that moves a client into the normal range of functioning. Statements C and D are plainly true: a raw score converts to a z score by z = (X minus mean) / SD, and z scores are standard scores with mean 0 and SD 1. Example: a survey of 20,000 shoppers finds that they prefer the new packaging designed by Riya's team by 0.2 per cent, with p below .01. The result is significant, but no manager would spend crores on a redesign for such a gain. Option b is right because it reverses the accepted priority. Exam trap: in an \"incorrect statement\" item, a and b are mirror images, so one of them must be false; students who equate \"significant\" with \"important\" choose a. Key note: which matters more is a judgement, but the research texts this item follows state A, so B is the incorrect statement and b stands.", "topic": "Managerial versus statistical significance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "People who are easily upset fall high on:", "options": {"a": "Introversion", "b": "Extraversion", "c": "Neuroticism", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: people who are easily upset score high on neuroticism. Neuroticism is the personality dimension of emotional instability: a tendency to feel anxiety, worry, moodiness, irritability and sadness, and to be thrown off balance by small stresses. Its opposite pole is emotional stability (calm, even-tempered, resilient). Hans Eysenck (1947) made it one of his two original superfactors, alongside extraversion, and later added psychoticism; his questionnaires (the MPI, EPI and EPQ) all score it. Eysenck linked high neuroticism to a reactive limbic system, the 'visceral brain', which sets off strong emotional arousal quickly and keeps it going. Neuroticism also survives as one of the Big Five factors of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985), whose NEO inventories break it into facets such as anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Example: Riya is shaken for days by a mild remark from her teacher, worries before every test and cries when plans change; she would score high on neuroticism, whether she is outgoing or shy. Option c is right because 'easily upset' describes emotional instability, which is exactly what the neuroticism dimension measures. Exam trap: students link being upset with being quiet and withdrawn and pick introversion. But introversion and extraversion concern sociability and arousal, and they are independent of neuroticism: Eysenck crossed the two dimensions to get four types, so there are stable and unstable introverts and stable and unstable extraverts.", "topic": "Neuroticism as emotional instability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The different forms of a gene are called:", "options": {"a": "Traits", "b": "Alleles", "c": "Characters", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the different forms of a gene are called alleles. A gene is a stretch of DNA at a fixed place (locus) on a chromosome that usually carries the code for a protein. Many genes come in more than one version, and each version is an allele. Because we receive one chromosome of each pair from each parent, every person carries two alleles of each gene on the autosomes. Two identical alleles make a person homozygous for that gene; two different ones make the person heterozygous. A dominant allele shows its effect even when only one copy is present, while a recessive allele shows only when both copies are recessive. Gregor Mendel (1866) worked this out in pea plants: his 'factors' for purple and white flowers were what we now call two alleles of one gene. The word is short for 'allelomorph' (from the Greek for 'one another' and 'form'), coined by William Bateson and Edith Saunders (1902). The alleles a person carries make up the genotype; the observable result is the phenotype. Example: Meera carries an A allele and an O allele of the ABO blood group gene; A is dominant over O, so her blood group is A, yet she can pass the O allele to her child. Option b is right because alleles are, by definition, alternative forms of the same gene at the same locus. Exam trap: traits and characters are what genes help to produce, the visible features of the phenotype, not the gene versions themselves; students who read 'different forms' as 'different features' fall for them.", "topic": "Alleles as gene variants", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis places too much emphasis on:", "options": {"a": "Unconscious Mind", "b": "Conscious Mind", "c": "Pre-Conscious Mind", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: psychoanalysis is criticised for placing too much emphasis on the unconscious mind. Sigmund Freud (1900) divided the mind in his topographic model into three levels: the conscious (what you are aware of right now), the preconscious (memories and facts you can bring to awareness easily, like your phone number) and the unconscious (wishes, memories and conflicts kept out of awareness, mostly by repression, which still drive behaviour). For Freud the unconscious was the largest and most important part, the submerged bulk of the iceberg: dreams, slips of the tongue, neurotic symptoms and even career choices were traced to hidden sexual and aggressive wishes formed in early childhood. Critics argued this went too far. Karl Popper (1963) said the theory could explain any outcome and so could never be shown to be false. Behaviourists said unconscious forces cannot be observed, and humanistic and cognitive psychologists said Freud neglected conscious choice, reasoning and present goals. Example: Arjun forgets his dentist appointment because he was busy, but a strict Freudian reads it as an unconscious wish to avoid pain, a reading Arjun can neither confirm nor refute. Option a is right because the stock criticism of psychoanalysis is its over-reliance on unconscious, unobservable causes (along with sexuality and early childhood). Exam trap: the preconscious sounds hidden too, but Freud gave it only a minor bridging role; and the conscious mind is what his critics wanted more weight placed on, the reverse of his emphasis.", "topic": "Criticism of psychoanalysis", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Peak experiences are central to:", "options": {"a": "Maslow's Theory", "b": "Rogers's Theory", "c": "Frankl's Theory", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: peak experiences are central to Abraham Maslow's theory. Abraham Maslow (1962) described peak experiences as brief, intense moments of joy, wonder, awe and unity, when a person feels whole, loses track of time and self, and sees the world as good and meaningful. He met them first in his study of self-actualisers, the healthy, creative people at the top of his hierarchy of needs, who reported them often; he later argued that almost everyone has them at some time. He wrote about them in Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) and Religions, Values, and Peak-Experiences (1964), and linked them to 'being cognition' (B-cognition), seeing things as they are rather than through one's needs. Peak experiences can come from love, music, nature, childbirth, creative work or prayer, and are often followed by a lasting change in outlook. Example: Kabir finishes a long trek at sunrise in the Himalayas and, for a few minutes, feels complete awe and oneness with everything around him; Maslow would call this a peak experience. Option a is right because the concept was named and developed by Maslow as part of his humanistic theory of self-actualisation. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1961) and Viktor Frankl (1946) are also humanistic or existential names linked to growth and meaning, so students mix them up; but Rogers's signature ideas are the fully functioning person and unconditional positive regard, and Frankl's is the will to meaning.", "topic": "Maslow's peak experiences", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "The book The Interpretation of Dreams was written by:", "options": {"a": "Adler", "b": "Freud", "c": "Smith", "d": "Anna O"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: The Interpretation of Dreams was written by Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1900) wrote Die Traumdeutung; it appeared in November 1899, though the title page bears the date 1900. He argued that every dream is the disguised fulfilment of a wish, often a forbidden sexual or aggressive one. What we remember on waking is the manifest content; the hidden meaning behind it is the latent content. The dream work turns latent into manifest content through condensation (several ideas fused into one image), displacement (feeling shifted from an important target to a trivial one), symbolisation, and secondary revision (the mind stitches the result into a story). Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious activities of the mind', and used his own dreams, such as the dream of Irma's injection, as worked examples. Example: Riya dreams that she misses a train; a Freudian would ask what wish the dream disguises, perhaps a wish to escape an exam she dreads. Option b is right because the book is Freud's founding work on dreams and the unconscious. Exam trap: Anna O sounds like a Freud author because her case is famous, but she was Bertha Pappenheim, the patient of Josef Breuer (1895) in Studies on Hysteria, who called the treatment 'the talking cure'; she wrote no book on dreams. Alfred Adler, another early analyst, is the other decoy.", "topic": "Freud's Interpretation of Dreams", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Past experiences are to Freud as fictional finalism is to:", "options": {"a": "Adler", "b": "Ringle", "c": "Cattell", "d": "Allport"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: fictional finalism is Alfred Adler's idea. Alfred Adler (1912), the founder of individual psychology, argued that people are pulled forward by goals rather than pushed by the past. Fictional finalism is the idea that behaviour is guided by imagined final goals: beliefs about the future that cannot be tested and may not be true, yet work as if they were, such as 'if I become rich I will be safe' or 'honest people are always rewarded'. Adler took the 'as if' idea from Hans Vaihinger (1911), whose The Philosophy of 'As If' held that people live by useful fictions. The final goal, usually a fiction of superiority or perfection born from childhood feelings of inferiority, organises a person's whole style of life. Freud's view is causal and deterministic: present behaviour is explained by past experiences, especially early childhood conflicts. Adler's view is teleological: behaviour is explained by where the person is heading. That is the analogy in the stem: past experiences are to Freud as imagined future goals are to Adler. Example: Kabir, small and teased as a child, lives by the private fiction that 'only toppers are respected', and that imagined goal, not the teasing itself, shapes his daily choices. Option a is right because fictional finalism is a core concept of Adler's individual psychology. Exam trap: Gordon Allport's (1937) functional autonomy also cuts motives loose from the past, so Allport tempts students; but it describes present motives running on their own, not guidance by imagined future goals.", "topic": "Adler's fictional finalism", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "SEMS are socially acquired and can satisfy:", "options": {"a": "One erg at a time", "b": "Two ergs at a time", "c": "Several ergs at a time", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: sems, the socially acquired dynamic traits, can satisfy several ergs at a time. Raymond Cattell (1957) divided the dynamic (motivational) traits into two kinds. Ergs are innate, biologically based drives, such as hunger, sex, fear (security), curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion; textbooks list about ten or eleven of them. Metanergs are learned through culture and experience. They include sentiments, large organised attitude systems centred on an important object such as career, family, religion or the self, and attitudes, specific interests in a particular course of action. Cattell himself later called sentiments 'sems', short for socially shaped ergic manifolds. Cattell linked them in a chain he called subsidiation: an attitude serves a sentiment, and a sentiment serves ergs. The whole web of links is the dynamic lattice, and its central feature is that one sentiment feeds many drives at once, while one erg can be fed by many sentiments. Example: Arjun's sentiment for his medical career satisfies self-assertion (status), security (a stable income), gregariousness (working with colleagues) and curiosity (learning about the body), all through the same set of attitudes, such as wanting to study tonight. Option c is right because, in the dynamic lattice, a learned sentiment normally serves several ergs at the same time. Exam trap: the stem looks as if it wants a fixed number, so 'one' or 'two' tempt students; but Cattell's point is that sentiments are general-purpose channels, with no fixed ratio of one sentiment to one or two drives.", "topic": "Cattell's ergs and sentiments", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Allport was interested in which of the following constructs?", "options": {"a": "Traits", "b": "Rumour", "c": "Prejudice", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport worked on all three constructs, traits, rumour and prejudice. Gordon Allport (1937) is best known as a founder of trait psychology. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation he treated traits as real neuropsychic structures and sorted them into cardinal (one ruling disposition), central (the handful that describe a person) and secondary (narrow preferences) traits; with Henry Odbert (1936) he listed 17,953 English trait words from the dictionary, the start of the lexical approach. During the Second World War he studied rumour. The Psychology of Rumor (1947), written with Leo Postman, gave a basic law: rumour strength varies with the importance of the topic times its ambiguity (R ~ i x a). Their serial reproduction studies showed that retold stories lose detail (levelling), keep a few vivid points (sharpening) and are reshaped to fit the teller's expectations (assimilation). In The Nature of Prejudice (1954) he defined prejudice as an antipathy based on a faulty and inflexible generalisation, and proposed the contact hypothesis: contact between groups reduces prejudice under equal status, common goals, cooperation and support from authorities. Example: a false story that exams are postponed races through Meera's hostel because the topic matters to everyone and the facts are unclear, just as Allport's rumour law predicts. Option d is right because each of the three constructs is a major Allport topic. Exam trap: students know Allport only as a trait theorist and pick 'Traits'; rumour and prejudice are listed precisely to catch that narrow reading.", "topic": "Allport's research interests", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Choose the incorrect statement:", "options": {"a": "Post traumatic growth is a positive construct", "b": "Post traumatic growth is harmful", "c": "Stress & trauma are entirely different", "d": "PTSD involves flashbacks"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the incorrect statement is that posttraumatic growth is harmful. Posttraumatic growth is the positive psychological change some people report after struggling with a highly stressful or traumatic event. Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun developed the idea in the mid-1990s and built the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (1996), which measures five domains: new possibilities, relating to others, personal strength, spiritual change and appreciation of life. Growth does not mean the trauma was good or that distress is absent; it comes from the struggle to rebuild one's beliefs afterwards, and it can sit alongside pain. It is therefore a positive construct, so statement A is true. Posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is a different outcome, and its intrusion symptoms (DSM-5 criterion B) include flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again; so statement D is true. Example: Meera loses her home in a flood; months later she still has bad dreams, but she also says she values her family more and has found a strength she did not know she had. Option b is right because calling posttraumatic growth harmful contradicts its definition as beneficial change after trauma. Exam trap: the word 'posttraumatic' makes students think of damage and PTSD, so 'harmful' sounds plausible; but growth is the positive side of the same experience. Key note: statement C is also shaky, since trauma is an extreme stressor and DSM-5 places PTSD under trauma- and stressor-related disorders, so stress and trauma overlap rather than being entirely different. But statement B contradicts the definition of posttraumatic growth outright, so b, the answer used here, stands.", "topic": "Posttraumatic growth", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The people with OCD perform:", "options": {"a": "Badly on procedural memory tasks", "b": "Better on procedural memory tasks", "c": "Average on procedural memory tasks", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: people with OCD have been found to perform better on procedural memory tasks. Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits, such as riding a bicycle or typing, shown in performance rather than conscious recall. It depends heavily on the basal ganglia, especially the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), working in loops with the frontal cortex. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) involves intrusive thoughts (obsessions) and repetitive acts done to reduce anxiety (compulsions), and brain imaging shows over-activity in a loop linking the orbitofrontal cortex, caudate and thalamus. Because this over-active fronto-striatal circuit also serves habits, researchers asked whether OCD might sharpen procedural learning. Robert Roth and colleagues (2004) compared 27 adults with OCD with 29 healthy controls: the OCD group beat controls on early trials of the pursuit rotor, a motor skill task, while their verbal declarative memory was normal. Many studies, by contrast, find people with OCD weaker on explicit memory, especially for non-verbal material, and low in confidence about their memory, which helps explain repeated checking. Example: Kabir, who has OCD, keeps doubting whether he locked the door, yet picks up a new hand-eye tracking task quickly. Option b is right because the reported finding is an advantage on procedural tasks, linked to striatal over-activation. Exam trap: since OCD is a disorder, students assume every memory system is impaired and pick 'badly'; the deficits lie mainly in explicit memory and memory confidence. Key note: the evidence is mixed; Roth's advantage faded on later trials, a PET study by Scott Rauch and colleagues (1997) found normal sequence learning that recruited medial temporal rather than striatal areas, and some studies report implicit learning deficits. But procedural memory is the only option with a reported advantage, so b, the answer used here, stands.", "topic": "OCD and procedural memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The phobia of being out of cellular phone contact is known as:", "options": {"a": "Nomophobia", "b": "Cellular Phobia", "c": "Escalaphobia", "d": "Emetophobia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the phobia of being out of mobile phone contact is called nomophobia. Nomophobia is short for 'no mobile phone phobia': anxiety or distress when a person cannot use their phone because it is lost, the battery is flat or there is no network, or when they are simply out of contact. The word came from a 2008 survey commissioned by the UK Post Office and run by YouGov, which found that about half of British mobile users felt anxious in these situations. Typical signs are checking the phone constantly, keeping it switched on in bed, carrying a charger everywhere and feeling panic when it is out of reach. It is not an official DSM-5 diagnosis; researchers treat it as a modern, situation-bound fear linked to problematic smartphone use and measure it with questionnaires such as the Nomophobia Questionnaire. Example: Kabir's phone dies on a long bus ride; his heart races and he keeps reaching for his pocket, worried that he is missing messages. That is nomophobia. Option a is right because nomophobia is the established term for the fear of being without mobile phone contact. Exam trap: 'cellular phobia' copies a word from the stem and sounds right, but it is not a recognised term; examiners rely on students choosing the most literal-sounding option. The other two are real phobia names with unrelated objects.", "topic": "Nomophobia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": {"a": "The exact cause of panic disorder is known", "b": "The exact cause of panic disorder is unknown", "c": "Panic doesn't occur unexpectedly", "d": "Panic disorder is more prevalent among children than adults"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the correct statement is that the exact cause of panic disorder is unknown. DSM-5 diagnoses panic disorder when a person has recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (sudden surges of intense fear that peak within minutes, with a pounding heart, breathlessness, dizziness and fear of dying or losing control), followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks or a change in behaviour, such as avoiding places. No single cause has been found. Instead, several models each explain part of the picture: a genetic vulnerability (it runs in families); the cognitive model of David Clark (1986), in which people catastrophically misinterpret harmless bodily sensations (a fast heartbeat means a heart attack), so fear spirals into panic; the suffocation false alarm theory of Donald Klein (1993), in which an over-sensitive brain alarm misreads rising carbon dioxide as suffocation; and the triple vulnerability model of David Barlow (2002), which combines a general biological, a general psychological and a specific learned vulnerability. Example: Arjun has his first attack on a crowded metro; afterwards he notices every flutter in his chest and avoids the metro. His psychologist can describe how the cycle works but cannot name one exact cause. Option b is right because panic disorder is understood through several interacting biological and psychological models, not one known cause. Exam trap: statement C reverses the diagnosis, since DSM-5 actually requires unexpected attacks; and statement D reverses the age pattern, since onset is usually in late adolescence or early adulthood and panic disorder is uncommon in young children.", "topic": "Causes of panic disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The catecholamine hypothesis explains the causes of:", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Stress", "c": "GAD", "d": "Depression"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the catecholamine hypothesis explains the causes of depression. Catecholamines are a family of monoamine neurotransmitters made from the amino acid tyrosine: dopamine, noradrenaline (norepinephrine) and adrenaline (epinephrine). Joseph Schildkraut (1965) proposed the catecholamine hypothesis of affective disorders: depression is linked to a functional deficiency of catecholamines, especially noradrenaline, at important brain synapses, and mania to an excess. William Bunney and John Davis (1965) put forward the same noradrenaline idea in the same year. The evidence came from drugs. Reserpine, a blood pressure medicine that depletes monoamines from nerve endings, made some patients depressed, whereas the first antidepressants raised monoamine levels: monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors block their breakdown and tricyclics block their reuptake. A sister idea, the serotonin (indoleamine) hypothesis, came from Alec Coppen (1967); together they form the monoamine hypothesis. Later research showed the story is too simple: antidepressants raise transmitter levels within hours, yet mood takes weeks to lift, so slower receptor changes and neuroplasticity are now thought to matter. Example: Meera's psychiatrist explains that her antidepressant boosts noradrenaline and serotonin at her synapses, an explanation that comes straight from the catecholamine and serotonin hypotheses. Option d is right because the hypothesis was framed to explain depression (and mania), the affective disorders. Exam trap: noradrenaline and adrenaline are also part of the stress and fight-or-flight response, so 'stress' and 'anxiety' tempt students; but the catecholamine hypothesis is a theory of mood disorder.", "topic": "Catecholamine hypothesis of depression", "fig": "Joseph Schildkraut"}, {"q": "The full form of SAD in context of psychopathology is:", "options": {"a": "Seasonal Affective Disorder", "b": "Somatic Affective Disorder", "c": "Seasonal Alarm Disorder", "d": "Somatic Affective Disorganization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: SAD stands for seasonal affective disorder. Seasonal affective disorder is depression that comes and goes with the seasons. In the usual winter pattern, low mood, low energy, oversleeping, craving for carbohydrates and weight gain begin in autumn or winter and lift in spring and summer, year after year. Norman Rosenthal and colleagues (1984) described and named it at the US National Institute of Mental Health. DSM-5 does not list SAD as a separate disorder; it is coded as recurrent major depressive disorder or bipolar disorder 'with seasonal pattern', a specifier that needs a regular link between episodes and a particular time of year, with full remission at another time, over at least two years. The leading explanation is reduced daylight, which shifts circadian rhythms and the timing of melatonin release, along with changes in serotonin. The main treatment is bright light therapy: sitting each day in front of a light box of about 10,000 lux, usually in the morning. Example: every November Riya, who studies in a northern city with short grey days, becomes tired and withdrawn and sleeps late; by March she feels like herself again. Option a is right because 'seasonal affective disorder' is the established name for this recurrent seasonal depression. Exam trap: the other options mix real words ('somatic', 'affective', 'alarm') into plausible acronyms, and 'somatic' points students towards somatic symptom disorders, which have nothing to do with the seasons.", "topic": "Seasonal affective disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The DSM-5 organizes personality disorders into:", "options": {"a": "1 Cluster", "b": "3 Clusters", "c": "4 Clusters", "d": "10 Clusters"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: DSM-5 organises personality disorders into 3 clusters. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) lists 10 specific personality disorders and groups them by shared features into three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is 'weird, wild and worried' for A, B and C. The clusters are a teaching and research convenience with limited validity, and many people meet criteria for disorders in more than one cluster. DSM-5 also offers, in Section III, an Alternative Model for Personality Disorders that rates impairment in personality functioning plus five pathological trait domains. Example: a clinician notes that Arjun is suspicious of everyone and keeps to himself, which points to Cluster A, while his cousin Kabir is anxious, clingy and afraid of disapproval, which points to Cluster C. Option b is right because the categorical DSM-5 system uses exactly three clusters, labelled A, B and C. Exam trap: 10 is the number of personality disorders, not the number of clusters, and students who remember '10' grab that option.", "topic": "DSM-5 personality disorder clusters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which personality disorder is characterized by exaggerated self image?", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic Personality Disorder", "b": "Histrionic Personality Disorder", "c": "Borderline Personality Disorder", "d": "Schizoid Personality Disorder"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: an exaggerated self-image is the hallmark of narcissistic personality disorder. Narcissistic personality disorder (NPD), one of DSM-5's Cluster B disorders, is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity (in fantasy or behaviour), need for admiration and lack of empathy, beginning by early adulthood. At least 5 of 9 criteria are needed: a grandiose sense of self-importance (exaggerating achievements and expecting to be seen as superior); fantasies of unlimited success, power or beauty; a belief that one is special and should mix only with high-status people; a need for excessive admiration; a sense of entitlement; exploiting others; lack of empathy; envy; and arrogant behaviour. The name comes from the Greek myth of Narcissus, who fell in love with his own reflection. Beneath the grandiosity self-esteem is often fragile, so criticism can trigger rage or shame. Example: Kabir tells everyone that his ordinary work project will change the industry, expects special treatment from his manager and cannot see why colleagues are hurt when he dismisses their work. Option a is right because a grandiose, inflated sense of self-importance, the exaggerated self-image of the stem, is the core feature of NPD. Exam trap: borderline personality disorder also has a 'self-image' criterion, but it is an unstable, shifting self-image (identity disturbance), not an exaggerated one; and histrionic patients seek attention through emotion and appearance, not through claims of superiority.", "topic": "Narcissistic personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Desensitizing is:", "options": {"a": "Operant conditioning in action", "b": "Classical conditioning in action", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Very harmful"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: desensitisation is classical conditioning in action. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, treats phobias and anxiety by counterconditioning. In classical conditioning terms, the feared object has become a conditioned stimulus that triggers anxiety. Therapy pairs that same stimulus with a new response that cannot coexist with anxiety, deep relaxation; Wolpe called this reciprocal inhibition, since a person cannot be relaxed and anxious at once. There are three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of situations from least to most frightening, each rated in SUDS (subjective units of disturbance, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each step in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. A forerunner was Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed a small boy called Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate food he liked. Example: Meera fears dogs; while relaxed she pictures a dog far away in a park, then a puppy across the room, and finally imagines patting a dog, staying calm at each image before moving on. Option b is right because the method works through new stimulus pairings (feared stimulus with relaxation), not through rewards or punishments. Exam trap: a therapy with graded steps sounds like operant shaping, so students pick 'operant' or 'both'; but desensitisation does not reinforce the client's behaviour, so it is a classical (respondent) technique.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation mechanism", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Cognitive therapy emphasizes on:", "options": {"a": "What People Think", "b": "What People Do", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "What People Observe"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive therapy emphasises what people think. Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck (1960s; set out fully in Cognitive Therapy and the Emotional Disorders, 1976), rests on the idea that it is not events themselves but the way we interpret them that shapes how we feel and act. Beck noticed that depressed patients had a stream of quick, negative automatic thoughts ('I always fail'), produced by deeper core beliefs or schemas ('I am worthless') and bent by systematic thinking errors such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising, all-or-nothing thinking and personalisation. In depression these form the cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world and the future. Therapist and client work as collaborators: they identify thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and replace them with more balanced ones. Albert Ellis (1955) built a parallel approach, first called rational therapy and now rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs using an ABC model. Example: Riya fails one test and thinks, 'I am stupid and will never pass'; her therapist helps her list the tests she has passed and rewrite the thought as 'I did badly on this one because I revised the wrong chapter'. Option a is right because cognitive therapy targets thoughts, beliefs and interpretations as the main source of distress. Exam trap: modern practice is usually cognitive-behavioural therapy, which works on both thoughts and actions, so 'Both' is a near miss; but the stem asks about cognitive therapy, whose defining emphasis is thinking.", "topic": "Focus of cognitive therapy", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "Which of the following belongs to the humanistic school?", "options": {"a": "Sartre", "b": "Buber", "c": "Kierkegaard", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: Sartre, Buber and Kierkegaard all belong to the humanistic (humanistic-existential) school. Humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, grew in the 1950s and 1960s around Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1951). Its sister approach, existential psychology, drew on existentialist philosophers, and most textbooks treat the two as one humanistic-existential school because both stress freedom, choice, responsibility, subjective experience and the whole person. Soren Kierkegaard (1844), the Danish philosopher often called the father of existentialism, wrote in The Concept of Anxiety of anxiety as 'the dizziness of freedom', and stressed the individual's task of choosing who to become. Martin Buber (1923), in I and Thou, contrasted the I-Thou relationship (meeting another person as a whole, genuine being) with the I-It relationship (treating them as an object), an idea Rogers drew on for the therapeutic encounter. Jean-Paul Sartre (1943), author of Being and Nothingness, held that we are 'condemned to be free' and, in his famous slogan, that existence precedes essence: we make ourselves through our choices. Rollo May, Ernest Angel and Henri Ellenberger brought these ideas into American psychology in Existence (1958). Example: when Kabir's counsellor tells him that he, not his parents or fate, is responsible for choosing his path, she is drawing on Sartre and Kierkegaard. Option d is right because each of the three supplied core ideas to the humanistic-existential school. Exam trap: students know only Maslow and Rogers as humanists and so reject philosophers' names; examiners list them to test the existential roots. Key note: strictly, these three are existentialist philosophers rather than humanistic psychologists, but textbooks group the humanistic and existential approaches as one school, so d, the answer used here, stands.", "topic": "Existentialist roots of humanistic psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Existential therapy focuses on the:", "options": {"a": "Self determination", "b": "Free will", "c": "Meaning", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: existential therapy focuses on self-determination, free will and meaning. Existential therapy is not one technique but an approach that helps clients face the basic givens of human life. Irvin Yalom (1980), in Existential Psychotherapy, named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Freedom here means that we are free to choose and therefore responsible for our lives; self-determination and free will are two names for this theme, and dodging responsibility (blaming parents, fate or circumstances) is seen as a source of anxiety and stuckness. Rollo May (1969), in Love and Will, stressed freedom, will and the anxiety that comes with choice. Meaning is the theme of Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy, developed in the 1930s and tested by his concentration camp experience, holds that the will to meaning is the primary human motive, and that meaning can be found in work, in love and in the attitude one takes towards suffering. The therapist works through an honest, here-and-now relationship rather than set techniques, helping the client accept responsibility and choose authentically. Example: Arjun feels his life is empty and blames his parents for his engineering degree; his existential therapist helps him see that he still chooses what to do next and what will make his life meaningful. Option d is right because self-determination, free will and meaning are all central existential themes. Exam trap: 'self-determination' also recalls the self-determination theory of motivation of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), which makes some students doubt option a; in existential therapy the word simply means the freedom and responsibility to choose.", "topic": "Themes of existential therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following deals with client's rights and responsibilities in context of counseling?", "options": {"a": "Contract", "b": "Covenant", "c": "Informed Consent", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: informed consent is the part of counselling that deals with the client's rights and responsibilities. Informed consent means the client agrees to counselling freely, after being told in plain language what it involves. Before counselling begins, the counsellor explains the nature, goals and likely length of the work, the methods, the counsellor's qualifications, fees, and the limits of confidentiality (for example, risk of serious harm to self or others, child abuse, or a court order). The client learns his or her rights: to ask questions, to refuse a technique and to end counselling at any time. Responsibilities are spelt out too: attending sessions, paying fees and working towards agreed goals. Informed consent is an ongoing process, revisited as the work changes, not a single signature. It appears in the American Counseling Association Code of Ethics (2014, section A.2) and in the American Psychological Association Ethics Code (Standard 10.01, informed consent to therapy). Example: before her first session, Meera is told that what she says stays private unless someone is in danger, what each session costs, and that she may stop whenever she wishes; only then does she agree. Option c is right because informed consent is the ethical process built around the client's rights and responsibilities. Exam trap: a counselling contract also mentions fees and goals, so students pick 'Contract' or 'All of these'; but a contract records the working arrangement, while rights and responsibilities are the content of informed consent.", "topic": "Informed consent in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In psychoanalytic counseling which of the following methods are used?", "options": {"a": "Free Association", "b": "Dream Analysis", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Interview Method"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: psychoanalytic counselling uses both free association and dream analysis. Psychoanalysis, the therapy of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), aims to make unconscious conflicts conscious so that the client can understand and work through them. Its methods are built to get round the mind's censorship. In free association the client, often lying on a couch with the analyst out of sight, follows the fundamental rule: say whatever comes to mind, however trivial, embarrassing or illogical. Freud adopted it in the 1890s after giving up hypnosis, because many patients could not be hypnotised. Where the flow of associations stops or swerves, the analyst sees resistance and a clue to hidden material. In dream analysis the client recalls a dream and free-associates to each part of it. In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road' to the unconscious: the remembered manifest content disguises a hidden latent wish through condensation, displacement and symbolisation. The analyst also interprets resistance and transference. Example: Kabir dreams of missing a train; associating to it, he reaches his fear of failing his father's expectations. Option c is right because free association and dream analysis are both core psychoanalytic techniques, so neither (A) nor (B) alone is complete. Exam trap: the interview is used by every school of counselling, from person-centred to behavioural, so it is not a distinctively psychoanalytic method.", "topic": "Psychoanalytic techniques", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "SUDS is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Systematic Desensitization", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Aversion", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: SUDS is associated with systematic desensitisation. SUDS stands for the Subjective Units of Disturbance (or Distress) Scale, a self-rating from 0 (completely calm) to 100 (the worst anxiety imaginable); a 0 to 10 version is also used. Joseph Wolpe, who developed systematic desensitisation (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958), devised the scale; it is usually cited to his The Practice of Behavior Therapy (1969). In desensitisation the client first learns deep muscle relaxation, then builds an anxiety hierarchy of 10 to 20 feared situations, each given a SUDS rating so they can be ranked from least to most frightening. While relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn and moves up only when its SUDS rating has dropped to a low level. Relaxation, being incompatible with anxiety, gradually replaces the fear: reciprocal inhibition. Example: Riya, afraid of lifts, rates looking at a lift 15, standing inside with the door open 40 and riding ten floors 90, then works up the list. Option a is right because SUDS was created to build and climb the desensitisation hierarchy. Exam trap: SUDS rates anxiety, so students assume every anxiety-related therapy uses it and choose 'All of these'; aversion therapy, which pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus, has no need for an anxiety ladder. Key note: SUDS is also used to track anxiety during flooding and other exposure therapies, so (B) has some basis; but aversion therapy does not use it, so 'All of these' fails, and systematic desensitisation is the classic pairing (IHBAS 2020 keyed the same item this way); a stands.", "topic": "SUDS in desensitisation", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Carl Rogers's theory is based on how many major propositions?", "options": {"a": "09", "b": "19", "c": "03", "d": "21"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Carl Rogers stated his theory of personality as 19 propositions. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), founder of client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, set them out in the chapter 'A Theory of Personality and Behavior' in Client-Centered Therapy (1951). The first proposition says that every individual exists in a continually changing world of experience of which he is the centre: the phenomenal field. Later propositions add that a person reacts to the field as he perceives it, not as it objectively is; that part of the field becomes differentiated as the self; that experiences consistent with the self-concept are accepted, while inconsistent ones are denied or distorted; and that this incongruence between self and experience produces tension, anxiety and defence. Under conditions of safety the person can admit such experiences and move towards becoming a fully functioning person. Example: Arjun sees himself as a calm person, so he denies his anger at his brother; in a safe, accepting therapy he can admit it, and his self-concept widens. Option b is right because the theory is stated in exactly 19 propositions. Exam trap: other Rogerian numbers sit close by: 3 core conditions (congruence, unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding) and 6 necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change (1957). Learn which number goes with which list.", "topic": "Rogers's 19 propositions", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "The concept of measurement scales was given by:", "options": {"a": "Stevens", "b": "Galton", "c": "Pearson", "d": "Spearman"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the concept of scales (levels) of measurement was given by S. S. Stevens. Stanley Smith Stevens (1906 to 1973), a Harvard psychophysicist, set it out in 'On the Theory of Scales of Measurement' (Science, 1946). He defined measurement as assigning numbers to objects or events according to rules, and argued that different rules create four scales, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names categories (blood group, the number on a cricketer's shirt). An ordinal scale adds order (class rank). An interval scale adds equal units but has an arbitrary zero (Celsius temperature, IQ). A ratio scale adds a true zero, so ratios are meaningful (weight, reaction time). Stevens tied each scale to its permissible statistics, which textbooks summarise as mode and chi-square for nominal data, median and rank correlation for ordinal, mean, SD and Pearson r for interval, and all statistics, including the coefficient of variation, for ratio. Example: in Meera's survey, gender is nominal, rank in class is ordinal, an IQ score is interval and monthly income is ratio. Option a is right because Stevens proposed the four-scale scheme. Exam trap: the other three are famous statisticians whose names appear beside the scales in textbooks (Pearson r for interval data, Spearman's rho for ordinal), but none of them classified the scales.", "topic": "Levels of measurement", "fig": "Stanley Smith Stevens"}, {"q": "The ratio scale has:", "options": {"a": "True Zero", "b": "Arbitrary Zero", "c": "Many Limitations", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the ratio scale has a true zero. A true (absolute) zero means zero amount of the thing measured: 0 kg is no weight, 0 seconds is no time, 0 errors is no errors. Because zero is real, ratios make sense: a reaction time of 600 milliseconds is twice one of 300, and Kabir, who earns 40,000 rupees a month, earns twice as much as Arjun at 20,000. Ratio is the highest of the four levels of measurement set out by Stanley Smith Stevens (1946): it has identity, order, equal intervals and a true zero, so it permits every statistic, including the geometric mean and the coefficient of variation. Examples in psychology are reaction time, number of correct answers, height, weight and income. An interval scale has equal units but an arbitrary zero chosen by convention. On the Celsius scale 0 degrees is the freezing point of water, not the absence of heat, so 20 degrees is not twice as hot as 10 degrees; IQ and calendar years work the same way. Option a is right because the true zero is the defining feature of a ratio scale. Exam trap: the arbitrary zero belongs to the interval scale, one level lower, and examiners swap the two.", "topic": "Ratio scale and true zero", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is an alternative for C-alpha?", "options": {"a": "Construct Reliability", "b": "Composite Reliability", "c": "Spearman Rho", "d": "Pearson's Coefficient"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: composite reliability is the standard alternative to Cronbach's alpha. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) estimates internal consistency, how well the items of a scale hang together, from the number of items and how strongly they covary. It assumes that every item measures the construct equally well (tau-equivalence, or equal loadings). When some items are much stronger than others, alpha tends to underestimate the true reliability. Composite reliability, set out by Charles Werts, Robert Linn and Karl Joreskog (1974) and made popular in structural equation modelling by Claes Fornell and David Larcker (1981), uses the factor loadings from a confirmatory factor analysis: it weights each item by how strongly it reflects the construct, then compares true-score variance with total variance. Values of about 0.70 or more are usually taken as acceptable. Roderick McDonald's omega (Test Theory: A Unified Treatment, 1999) is essentially the same index for a one-factor scale. Example: Riya's five-item stress scale has two weak items; alpha comes out at 0.72, but composite reliability, giving the strong items more weight, is 0.76. Option b is right because composite reliability is the reliability index used in place of alpha in factor-based research. Exam trap: Spearman's rho and Pearson's r are correlations between two variables, not measures of a scale's internal consistency. Key note: some structural equation modelling texts (Joseph Hair and colleagues' Multivariate Data Analysis) call the same index 'construct reliability', so option a is close; but 'composite reliability' is the usual name for the alternative to alpha, so b stands.", "topic": "Composite reliability versus alpha", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The type of validity which is concerned with the question of whether the results support the theory behind the research is:", "options": {"a": "Concurrent Validity", "b": "Construct Validity", "c": "External Validity", "d": "Face Validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: construct validity asks whether the results support the theory behind the research. A construct is an unobservable theoretical idea, such as anxiety, intelligence or self-esteem. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that a test measures a construct only if its scores behave the way the theory says they should, fitting a nomological network of predicted relations. Evidence comes from several sources. Convergent validity: the test correlates with other measures of the same construct. Discriminant validity: it does not correlate highly with measures of different constructs. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) checked both together in the multitrait-multimethod matrix. Experimental evidence also counts: if theory says anxiety rises before an exam, scores should rise too. Example: Kabir's new test anxiety scale correlates .65 with an established anxiety inventory and only .10 with a vocabulary test, and scores rise on the morning of an exam, as the theory predicts, so its construct validity is supported. Option b is right because construct validity is the only option defined by agreement between the results and the underlying theory. Exam trap: concurrent and predictive validity check a test against a practical criterion, not against theory, and external validity concerns generalisation; examiners rely on students blurring the different kinds of validity.", "topic": "Construct validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher has developed two equivalent versions of a test and has administered them on the same set of participants on two different occasions. He is using _____ method of reliability.", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Split-half", "c": "Path Analysis", "d": "Alternative-forms"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: giving two equivalent versions of a test to the same people is the alternate-forms (parallel or equivalent forms) method of reliability. The two forms are built from the same blueprint: same content areas, same number and type of items, same difficulty, and similar means and variances. Both are given to the same group and the two sets of scores are correlated. If they are given in one sitting, the correlation is the coefficient of equivalence, which reflects error from content sampling (which particular items happened to be chosen). When, as in this stem, there is a time gap between the two, the correlation is the coefficient of stability and equivalence, which captures both content error and error from changes over time, so it is usually the strictest estimate. Parallel forms also reduce practice and memory effects, since the second test has new items. Example: Meera builds Form A and Form B of a 40-item arithmetic test, gives Form A to a class in July and Form B in August, and finds r = .84. Option d is right because two equivalent versions given to the same people define the alternate-forms method. Exam trap: the 'two occasions' wording pulls students towards test-retest, but test-retest uses the same form both times; the deciding detail is 'two equivalent versions'.", "topic": "Alternate-forms reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to R. Cattell in the 16PF, \"Suspicious vs Trusting\" describes:", "options": {"a": "Vigilance", "b": "Self-Reliance", "c": "Dominance", "d": "Liveliness"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the 16PF, 'Suspicious versus Trusting' is factor L, Vigilance. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis of trait ratings, questionnaires and objective tests to find 16 source traits, the basic dimensions underlying personality, and built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, first published 1949; fifth edition 1993) to measure them. Each factor is bipolar and reported as a sten score from 1 to 10. Low scorers on Vigilance are trusting, unsuspecting and accepting, ready to take people at their word. High scorers are vigilant, suspicious, sceptical and wary, expecting to be misled. The other factors are A Warmth, B Reasoning, C Emotional Stability, E Dominance, F Liveliness, G Rule-Consciousness, H Social Boldness, I Sensitivity, M Abstractedness, N Privateness, O Apprehension, Q1 Openness to Change, Q2 Self-Reliance, Q3 Perfectionism and Q4 Tension. Example: Arjun double-checks every bill and assumes the shopkeeper is cheating him; he would score high on factor L. Option a is right because Vigilance is the factor whose poles are trusting and suspicious. Exam trap: the factor letters are not consecutive (there is no D, J or K), and the label 'Vigilance' does not obviously suggest trust, so students mismatch it with Dominance or Self-Reliance.", "topic": "16PF factor L, Vigilance", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Murray is associated with:", "options": {"a": "TAT", "b": "Herman Melville Studies", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Rorschach Test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: Henry Murray is associated with both the TAT and Herman Melville studies. Henry Murray (1893 to 1988), of the Harvard Psychological Clinic, created the Thematic Apperception Test with Christiana Morgan (1935). The person tells a story about each of a series of ambiguous pictures (31 cards in the standard set, one of them blank), saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The stories are read for the hero's needs and for the press (environmental forces) acting on the hero, following Murray's theory of personology in Explorations in Personality (1938), with needs such as achievement, affiliation and dominance. Murray was also a lifelong scholar of the novelist Herman Melville (1819 to 1891), whose work he read as a study in unconscious conflict. He edited Melville's novel Pierre (1949) with a long psychological introduction and wrote the essay 'In Nomine Diaboli' (1951) on Moby-Dick. Example: Kabir, shown a boy gazing at a violin, tells of a child forced to practise who dreams of escape, hinting at a need for autonomy. Option c is right because both statements describe Murray's work, so neither (A) nor (B) alone is complete. Exam trap: the Rorschach is the other famous projective test, so it sits beside the TAT in options, but it belongs to Hermann Rorschach, not to Murray.", "topic": "Henry Murray's contributions", "fig": "Henry Murray"}, {"q": "Rorschach is the author of which of the following book/s?", "options": {"a": "Psychodiagnostik", "b": "Inkblot Test", "c": "Projection Theory", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: Hermann Rorschach wrote Psychodiagnostik. Hermann Rorschach (1884 to 1922), a Swiss psychiatrist, published Psychodiagnostik in 1921, the monograph that introduced his inkblot method. He tried many blots on patients and healthy people and settled on 15, but the publisher would print only ten, so the test has ten cards. The ten bilaterally symmetrical cards are always shown in the same order: five achromatic, black and grey (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two black and red (II, III) and three multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). The person says what each blot might be, and responses are scored for location (whole blot or a detail), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading) and content. Rorschach died the year after the book appeared, aged 37, so others developed the scoring, most influentially John Exner with his Comprehensive System (1974). Example: Meera sees a bat in Card V and two dancers in Card II; the scorer codes where in the blot she looked and what made it look that way. Option a is right because Psychodiagnostik is the only real book among the options. Exam trap: 'Inkblot Test' is the name of his test, not the title of a book, and the language of 'projection' and 'projective methods' came from other people.", "topic": "Rorschach's Psychodiagnostik", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "The qualitative and quantitative research paradigms are:", "options": {"a": "Complementary to each other", "b": "Contradictory to each other", "c": "Non scientific procedures", "d": "Not applicable in direct sciences"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: qualitative and quantitative research paradigms are complementary to each other. Quantitative research measures variables with numbers, uses larger and often probability samples, tests hypotheses statistically and aims at prediction and generalisation (experiments, surveys). Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and context through words and observation, uses small purposive samples and interprets data inductively (interviews, ethnography, grounded theory, case studies). Each answers questions the other cannot: numbers show how much and how often, words show how and why. Combining the two in one study is mixed methods research, set out by John Creswell and Vicki Plano Clark (2007): for example, a survey followed by interviews that explain its findings. Using several methods to cross-check a conclusion is triangulation (Norman Denzin, 1978); when both kinds of evidence agree, confidence in the finding rises. Example: Arjun surveys 300 college students and finds that 40 per cent feel lonely, then interviews 12 of them to learn what loneliness feels like and what makes it worse; together the two give a fuller picture than either alone. Option a is right because the two approaches answer different parts of a question and are routinely combined. Exam trap: in the 'paradigm wars' of the 1980s some writers argued that the two were incompatible, which makes 'contradictory' sound plausible; the modern consensus is that they complement each other.", "topic": "Qualitative and quantitative paradigms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a step of research?", "options": {"a": "Hypothesis Testing", "b": "Data Manipulating", "c": "Discussion of Findings", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: data manipulating is not a step of research. A research study follows an ordered series of steps. The researcher identifies and defines a problem, reviews the literature, frames hypotheses, chooses a design and a sample, collects data, analyses them and tests the hypotheses, interprets and discusses the findings, and writes the report, which often raises the next problem. C. R. Kothari's Research Methodology lays out this same flow. Data manipulation, in the sense of altering, selecting or inventing data so that results come out as hoped, is not a step but research misconduct. Misconduct is usually defined as fabrication (making up data), falsification (changing data, procedures or results so that the record misrepresents the research) and plagiarism, often shortened to FFP. Example: Riya finds that her mindfulness group did no better than the controls; quietly dropping the three participants who spoiled her result to report p < .05 would be falsification, whereas reporting the null result honestly is the discussion step. Option b is right because manipulating data corrupts research rather than forming part of it. Exam trap: researchers do manipulate the independent variable in an experiment, which is legitimate design; the item is about manipulating data, which is not. Key note: in statistics software 'data manipulation' can also mean routine cleaning, recoding and transforming of data, which is part of analysis; read here as falsifying data, it is not a step, so b stands.", "topic": "Steps of research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which law deals with the sample selection?", "options": {"a": "Law of large numbers", "b": "Law of small numbers", "c": "Law of representative numbers", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the law of large numbers deals with sample selection. Sampling theory, as taught in Indian statistics and research methods texts, rests on two laws. The law of statistical regularity says that a reasonably large sample drawn at random from a population will, on average, have the characteristics of that population. The law of inertia of large numbers says that large groups are more stable than small ones: in a big sample, unusual cases in one direction tend to be balanced by those in the other, so errors cancel out. Both are informal versions of the law of large numbers, first proved (for proportions) by Jacob Bernoulli in Ars Conjectandi (published 1713): as a random sample grows, its mean or proportion gets closer and closer to the population value. This is why researchers prefer larger random samples. Example: if Kabir picks 5 students at random from a college of 2,000, their average height may be far off; with 200 students it will be very close to the true college average. Option a is right because the law of large numbers justifies drawing conclusions about a population from a random sample. Exam trap: the 'law of small numbers' is not a sampling law but a bias, described by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1971), of wrongly expecting small samples to mirror the population. Key note: the laws usually named in research methods texts are the law of statistical regularity and the law of inertia of large numbers; of the options only the law of large numbers covers them, so a stands.", "topic": "Laws of sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following violates the ethical principles of research?", "options": {"a": "Neutralising Missing Data", "b": "Standardizing Data", "c": "Removing Outliers", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these violates the ethical principles of research. All three are standard steps in data preparation, the stage between collecting data and analysing them, as described in Naresh Malhotra's Marketing Research. Neutralising missing data means treating missing responses, for example by substituting a neutral value, usually the mean of that variable, so the case can still be used; the alternatives are imputing a value from the person's other answers, casewise deletion and pairwise deletion. Standardising data is a scale transformation, such as converting raw scores to z scores (mean 0, SD 1) so that variables measured in different units can be compared or combined. Removing outliers means screening for extreme values, such as an age typed as 210, and deleting or adjusting them for a stated reason. Each is ethical when done by a rule decided in advance and reported openly. Research ethics is violated by fabrication, falsification and plagiarism, and by harm to participants, deception without debriefing, breach of confidentiality or the lack of informed consent. Example: Meera replaces four missing items with the item mean, converts scores to z scores and drops one impossible reaction time of 0.01 seconds, noting each step in her method section. Option d is right because each listed step is a legitimate, reportable part of data preparation. Exam trap: words like 'removing' and 'neutralising' sound like tampering, but open data cleaning is not misconduct; hidden or selective handling is. Key note: removing outliers selectively just to reach significance would be falsification, but as stated each is a legitimate step, so 'None of these' stands.", "topic": "Ethics of data preparation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of grade correlation?", "options": {"a": "Spearman", "b": "Pearson", "c": "Adams", "d": "Galton"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: Charles Spearman gave the concept of grade correlation. Charles Spearman (1863 to 1945), the British psychologist also known for the g factor, introduced rank correlation in 'The proof and measurement of association between two things' (American Journal of Psychology, 1904). Instead of using raw scores, each person is ranked on both variables and the differences between the two ranks (D) are used: rho = 1 minus 6 times the sum of D squared, divided by N(N squared minus 1). When the ranks are expressed as grades, that is, percentile positions within the group, the same coefficient is called grade correlation, and the name, though now rare, is still in use. It suits ordinal data, such as class ranks or judges' ratings, and relations that rise or fall steadily without being straight lines, and it is less affected by outliers than Pearson's r. Example: two judges rank ten Bharatanatyam dancers; Arjun works out the rank differences and gets rho = .85, showing strong agreement. Option a is right because grade (rank) correlation comes from Spearman. Exam trap: Karl Pearson is the name most linked with correlation, so students pick him, but his product-moment r (1896) uses actual scores, not ranks or grades. Key note: Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) had earlier worked with 'grades' (percentile ranks), and Pearson later wrote on correlation from grades; but textbooks credit grade (rank) correlation to Spearman, so a stands.", "topic": "Spearman's grade correlation", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "A negative correlation between X and Y can be referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Anti Correlation", "b": "Inverse Correlation", "c": "False Correlation", "d": "Both (A) & (B)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a negative correlation can be called both an anticorrelation and an inverse correlation. A correlation describes how two variables move together. In a negative correlation, high scores on X go with low scores on Y: as one rises, the other falls. The coefficient r lies between 0 and minus 1, and the scatterplot slopes downwards from left to right. Minus 1 is a perfect negative relation; values near 0 mean little linear relation. The sign shows direction only, and strength is shown by size, so r = minus .80 is stronger than r = plus .30. Inverse correlation is the everyday textbook synonym: the two variables are inversely related. Anticorrelation is the same idea, used especially in physics and brain imaging; Michael Fox and colleagues (2005) showed that activity in the brain's default mode network is anticorrelated with task-positive attention networks, falling when they rise. Example: the more hours Riya spends on her phone at night, the fewer hours she sleeps; phone use and sleep are negatively, or inversely, correlated. Option d is right because both (A) and (B) are accepted names for a negative correlation, so neither alone is complete. Exam trap: 'false correlation' sounds like a technical term, but the real near-miss is spurious correlation, a term Karl Pearson (1897) coined for misleading correlations between ratios, now used for any correlation produced by a third variable or by chance, which can be positive or negative.", "topic": "Names for negative correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not associated with the t-test?", "options": {"a": "p-Value", "b": "Critical Value", "c": "Effect Size", "d": "Mean Distortion"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: mean distortion is not associated with the t-test; it is not a statistical term at all. The t-test, published by William Sealy Gosset under the pen name 'Student' (1908) while he worked at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, compares means: one sample against a known value, two independent groups, or the same people measured twice. The other three options are all part of using it. The researcher computes t, the difference between means divided by its standard error. This value is compared with the critical value from the t table for the chosen alpha level (usually .05) and the degrees of freedom; if t exceeds it, the null hypothesis is rejected. Software reports the p value, the probability of a difference at least this large if the null hypothesis were true. Because a significant t says nothing about size, the effect size is reported too, usually Cohen's d, the mean difference in SD units, with Jacob Cohen's (1988) benchmarks of 0.2 small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large. Example: Kabir finds that students who revise with flashcards outscore others, t(58) = 2.40, p = .02, d = 0.63, a medium to large effect. Option d is right because mean distortion is an invented phrase with no place in t-test procedure. Exam trap: examiners place one made-up term among real ones; check each option against what you know rather than picking the one that sounds least familiar or most technical.", "topic": "t-test concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following tests is related with the goodness of fit concept?", "options": {"a": "CMH Test", "b": "Chi-Square Test", "c": "McNemar Test", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the chi-square test is the test related to goodness of fit. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for frequency data. A goodness of fit test asks whether the observed frequencies in the categories of one variable match the frequencies expected under some hypothesis. The statistic is chi-square = the sum, over all categories, of (O minus E) squared divided by E, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of categories minus 1. A small chi-square means a good fit; a large one means the data depart from the expected pattern. A classic use is testing the 3:1 ratio of dominant to recessive pea plants predicted by Gregor Mendel (1866). Chi-square also serves as a test of independence for two categorical variables in a contingency table. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four tea brands they prefer; equal preference predicts 30 per brand, and her observed counts of 45, 30, 25 and 20 give chi-square = 11.67 with 3 degrees of freedom, above the .05 critical value of 7.81, so preferences are not equal. Option b is right because chi-square goodness of fit is the standard test comparing observed with expected frequencies. Exam trap: the CMH and McNemar tests also produce chi-square statistics, so 'All of these' tempts, but neither tests the fit of one distribution to a hypothesised one.", "topic": "Chi-square goodness of fit", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-hfo": [{"q": "In the year 1890 (printed as \"1989\"), which of the following books was published by William James?", "concept": "The answer is c: William James published The Principles of Psychology in 1890. William James (1890), often called the father of American psychology, spent about 12 years writing this two-volume work of nearly 1,400 pages, and it laid the groundwork for functionalism, the American school that asks what the mind is for rather than what it is made of. Its best-known chapters describe the stream of consciousness (thought is personal, always changing, continuous and selective), habit as \"the enormous fly-wheel of society\", the self split into the knowing \"I\" and the known \"Me\" (material, social and spiritual selves), and the theory of emotion that James shared with Carl Lange (1885). Students found the big book heavy, so James wrote a one-volume abridgement, Psychology: Briefer Course (1892), which they nicknamed \"the Jimmy\". Example: when Meera notices that her thoughts while studying drift from an exam to dinner to a film song without any clear breaks, she is describing what James called the stream of consciousness. Option c is right because The Principles of Psychology is the title James published in 1890. Exam trap: the fillers borrow real psychology words; attention and perception are chapters inside the Principles, not a separate book, and the title should not be confused with Wilhelm Wundt's Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874). Key note: the paper prints the year as \"1989\", a misprint for 1890; it does not change the answer, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Basics of Psychology is a filler title; William James wrote no book by this name, and his short textbook was Psychology: Briefer Course (1892).", "b": "Pretext of Psychology is a made-up title with no link to William James; it only sounds like a textbook name.", "d": "Attention and perception are famous chapters inside The Principles of Psychology (1890), but William James never published a separate book called Attention & Perception."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the goal of psychology?", "concept": "The answer is d: description, explanation and prediction are all goals of psychology. Introductory texts list four goals of psychology as a science: to describe, explain, predict and control (or influence) behaviour and mental processes. Description means observing carefully and recording what happens, such as how often a child hits classmates. Explanation means finding the causes, usually by building and testing a theory: is the hitting copied from television, rewarded by attention, or a sign of frustration? Prediction means using that knowledge to forecast when the behaviour will occur again. Control, the fourth goal, means using it to change behaviour for the better, as therapists, teachers and health workers do. Each goal builds on the one before: you cannot explain what you have not described, and a good explanation allows accurate prediction. Basic research mainly serves the first three goals; applied research adds control. Example: Arjun, a school psychologist, notes that Kabir disrupts class mainly during maths (description), links this to fear of failure (explanation), expects trouble before the next maths test (prediction) and plans extra support to prevent it (control). Option d is right because description, explanation and prediction are each recognised goals, so all three listed options qualify. Exam trap: students who remember one headline goal, often prediction or understanding, pick a single option; when every listed item is a true goal, \"All of the above\" is the answer, and the absence of control from the list does not change that.", "traps": {"a": "Description (observing and recording behaviour accurately) is a goal of psychology, but it is only the first of four; picking it alone leaves out explanation and prediction.", "b": "Explanation (finding the causes of behaviour through theory and research) is a goal, but choosing it alone leaves out description and prediction, which are also listed.", "c": "Prediction (forecasting when a behaviour will occur) is a goal, but choosing it alone ignores description and explanation, which are equally goals."}}, {"q": "In which of the following, the behaviour is controlled by the consequences?", "concept": "The answer is a: in operant conditioning behaviour is controlled by its consequences. Operant (instrumental) conditioning is learning in which the likelihood of a voluntary behaviour changes according to what follows it. Edward Thorndike (1898) put hungry cats in puzzle boxes; they escaped faster over trials because responses followed by food were \"stamped in\", a principle he later stated as the law of effect (1911). B. F. Skinner (1937, a year before The Behavior of Organisms) coined the word \"operant\" for a response that operates on the environment, and studied rats and pigeons in the operant chamber (the Skinner box). Reinforcement raises the rate of a behaviour: positive reinforcement adds something pleasant, negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant. Punishment lowers the rate, and extinction follows when reinforcement stops. Example: Riya's parents praise her each time she finishes her homework before dinner, and soon she does it every day; the praise, a consequence, controls the behaviour. Option a is right because consequences (reinforcement and punishment) are the defining cause of change in operant learning. Exam trap: classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) is also learning, but there the response is elicited by a stimulus that comes before it; the dog salivates to the bell whether or not anything follows. Remember: classical is about what comes before (stimulus to stimulus), operant about what comes after (response to consequence).", "traps": {"b": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus so that the neutral one comes to elicit a response; the response is triggered by what comes before it, not controlled by consequences.", "c": "Consequent conditioning is not a recognised term; it borrows the word consequence from the stem, but the learning controlled by consequences is called operant or instrumental conditioning.", "d": "None of these fails because operant conditioning, option a, is exactly the type of learning defined by control through consequences."}}, {"q": "To measure perceived intensity Stevens used:", "concept": "The answer is b: Stanley Smith Stevens measured perceived intensity by magnitude estimation. Psychophysics links the physical strength of a stimulus to the strength of the sensation. Gustav Fechner (1860) had assumed that each just noticeable difference is an equal step of sensation and derived a logarithmic law. Stanley Smith Stevens (1957) asked people directly instead. In magnitude estimation an observer is given a standard (say a light called \"10\") and then assigns every other stimulus a number in proportion to how strong it feels: a light that seems twice as bright gets \"20\". Plotting these numbers against physical intensity gave Stevens' power law, S = kI^n, where the exponent n differs by sense. For brightness n is about 0.33 (compression: a big rise in light feels like a small rise in brightness); for electric shock it is about 3.5 (expansion: a small rise in current feels far stronger). Example: in a lab, Kabir hears a tone called \"10\" and calls a louder one \"30\" because it seems three times as loud; that is magnitude estimation. Option b is right because assigning numbers to felt strength is the method on which Stevens built his power law. Exam trap: magnitude production sounds like a twin, but it reverses the task: the observer is given a number and adjusts the stimulus until it feels that strong. Key note: Stevens also used magnitude production and cross-modality matching as cross-checks, so \"Both\" has a case; standard perception textbooks (for example E. Bruce Goldstein's Sensation and Perception) name magnitude estimation as his method, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Magnitude production is the reverse procedure, in which the observer is given a number and adjusts the stimulus to match it; Stanley Smith Stevens used it only as a cross-check, not as his main measure.", "c": "Both would need the two methods to be equally Stevens' measure of perceived intensity; textbooks name magnitude estimation, with production and cross-modality matching as checks.", "d": "None of these fails because magnitude estimation, option b, is the method textbooks credit to Stevens."}}, {"q": "Haptic memory involves:", "concept": "The answer is c: haptic memory involves both the cutaneous and the kinaesthetic subsystems. Haptic memory is the brief sensory store for touch, the touch counterpart of iconic memory for vision and echoic memory for sound, the two stores Ulric Neisser (1967) named. George Sperling's (1960) partial-report studies showed how much the brief visual store holds; touch has been studied far less. Everything turns on what \"haptic\" means. Jack Loomis and Susan Lederman (1986) divided touch into the cutaneous subsystem, receptors in the skin that signal pressure, vibration, texture and temperature, and the kinaesthetic subsystem, receptors in muscles, tendons and joints that signal limb position and movement. Perception through the skin alone, with the hand still, is tactile perception; haptic perception uses both subsystems together, as when you actively explore an object with moving fingers. Haptic memory therefore briefly holds information from both channels after contact. Example: Meera reaches into her bag without looking and finds her keys by their cold metal edges (cutaneous) and by the shape her fingers trace as they move round them (kinaesthetic); both kinds of information are held for a moment as she recognises the bunch. Option c is right because haptic information, and so haptic memory, combines skin and movement signals. Exam trap: students equate \"haptic\" with skin touch alone and pick a, or with body movement alone and pick b; haptic means the active combination of both.", "traps": {"a": "The cutaneous subsystem (skin receptors for pressure, vibration and temperature) is one part of haptic memory; alone it describes tactile information and leaves out the kinaesthetic part.", "b": "The kinaesthetic subsystem (receptors in muscles, tendons and joints for position and movement) is the other part; alone it leaves out the skin input that haptic memory also holds.", "d": "None of these fails because both listed subsystems feed haptic memory, so option c is right."}}, {"q": "As per which rule does the cellular basis of learning involve strengthening of a synapse?", "concept": "The answer is a: Hebb's rule says the cellular basis of learning is the strengthening of a synapse. Donald Hebb (1949), in The Organization of Behavior, proposed how experience could change the brain. His postulate: when an axon of cell A is near enough to excite cell B and repeatedly or persistently takes part in firing it, some growth process or metabolic change occurs so that A's efficiency in firing B increases. In plain words, the connection between neurons that are active together grows stronger; a later paraphrase, often credited to Carla Shatz (1992), is \"cells that fire together wire together\". Groups of neurons linked in this way form cell assemblies that hold a memory. Physiological support came with long-term potentiation (LTP): Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) stimulated a pathway into the rabbit hippocampus at high frequency and found that the synaptic response stayed larger for hours afterwards. The NMDA receptor, which opens only when the sending and receiving cells are active together, is Hebb's rule in molecular form. Example: each time Arjun hears the word \"chai\" while smelling it, the neurons for the word and the smell fire together and their link strengthens, until the word alone brings the smell to mind. Option a is right because the rule that ties learning to synaptic strengthening is Hebb's. Exam trap: Charles Sherrington (1897) coined the word \"synapse\", so his name tempts students, but he studied reflexes and the integrative action of the nervous system, not a rule of learning.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Sherrington coined the word synapse (1897) and shared the 1932 Nobel Prize for work on neurons and reflexes, but there is no Sherrington's rule of synaptic learning.", "c": "William James wrote about habit and pathways in the brain (The Principles of Psychology, 1890), but no James's rule exists; the rule of synaptic strengthening is Hebb's.", "d": "None of these fails because Hebb's rule, option a, is exactly the principle the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the part of hippocampus?", "concept": "The answer is a: field CA3 is a part of the hippocampus. The hippocampus proper is also called the cornu ammonis (Ammon's horn), because in cross-section it curls like a ram's horn; CA stands for cornu ammonis. Rafael Lorente de No (1934) divided it into fields CA1, CA2, CA3 and CA4, the last now usually treated as the hilus of the dentate gyrus. Information flows through the trisynaptic circuit: from the entorhinal cortex along the perforant path to the dentate gyrus, from the dentate gyrus along the mossy fibres to CA3, and from CA3 along the Schaffer collaterals to CA1, which sends output back towards the cortex. CA3 is special because its cells have rich recurrent connections with one another, which is thought to let a partial cue reactivate a whole stored pattern (pattern completion). CA1 is very sensitive to loss of oxygen: patient R.B., described by Stuart Zola-Morgan, Larry Squire and David Amaral (1986), lost CA1 cells after an interruption of blood supply to the brain and became amnesic. Example: when Riya smells jasmine and the whole scene of her cousin's wedding comes back, CA3's pattern completion is thought to play a part. Option a is right because CA3 is a named field of Ammon's horn. Exam trap: the distractors scramble the same letters (C3A, AC3); the correct form is always CA followed by a number from 1 to 4.", "traps": {"b": "Field C3A scrambles the letters of a real label; no hippocampal field has this name, since the fields are CA1 to CA4 (cornu ammonis plus a number).", "c": "Field AC3 reverses the letters; hippocampal fields are written CA (cornu ammonis) followed by the number, so AC3 does not exist.", "d": "None of these fails because field CA3, option a, is a genuine part of the hippocampus."}}, {"q": "The process by which short term memories are converted into long term memories is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: consolidation is the process by which short-term memories are converted into long-term memories. A new memory is fragile at first and easily disrupted; consolidation makes it stable. Georg Muller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) named the process after finding that learning a second list soon after the first disrupted recall of the first, as if the first trace had not yet set. Two levels are now distinguished. Synaptic (cellular) consolidation takes minutes to hours and needs new protein synthesis at the synapse. Systems consolidation takes weeks to years, as the hippocampus gradually hands the memory over to the cortex. Evidence: a head injury or electroconvulsive therapy disrupts the most recent memories most, while older memories survive (Theodule Ribot's law, 1881: the new perishes before the old). Patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), whose medial temporal lobes were removed by the surgeon William Scoville in 1953, could no longer form new long-term declarative memories (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), though most older memories were spared. Sleep after learning also aids consolidation. Example: Kabir revises his notes the night before an exam; if he sleeps well, more of them are consolidated and still there in the morning. Option b is right because consolidation is the technical name for this conversion. Exam trap: potentiation, as in long-term potentiation (LTP), is a cellular mechanism that may underlie consolidation, not the name of the process itself.", "traps": {"a": "Elongation is not a memory term; nothing in memory research is called elongation, so it is a filler.", "c": "Potentiation, as in long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo, 1973), is a lasting strengthening of synapses, a likely mechanism for consolidation rather than the name of the process.", "d": "Both (A) and (B) fails because elongation is not a memory process; only consolidation, option b, names the conversion."}}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of self-actualization?", "concept": "The answer is a: Abraham Maslow gave the concept of self-actualisation. Abraham Maslow (1943), in \"A Theory of Human Motivation\" and later in Motivation and Personality (1954), arranged needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and at the top self-actualisation, the need to become everything one is capable of becoming (\"What a man can be, he must be\"). The lower deficiency needs must be reasonably met before the growth need of self-actualisation drives behaviour. Maslow studied people he judged to be self-actualisers, such as Abraham Lincoln, Thomas Jefferson, Albert Einstein and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described them as accepting of self and others, spontaneous, problem-centred, independent and prone to peak experiences. Example: Riya has a secure job, a loving family and respect at work, yet in her thirties she starts learning Hindustani classical music simply to grow as a person; Maslow would call this self-actualisation. Option a is right because textbooks credit self-actualisation, as the peak of the need hierarchy, to Maslow. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1951) also wrote about an actualising tendency, so examiners hope you will pick b. Key note: Kurt Goldstein (1939, The Organism) coined the term, and Carl Rogers built his theory on an actualising tendency, but textbooks credit the concept as a need-hierarchy idea to Maslow, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Rogers (1951) described an actualising tendency and the fully functioning person, a closely related idea, but self-actualisation as the top of a needs hierarchy is credited to Abraham Maslow.", "c": "Franklin names no psychologist linked to this concept; if it means Viktor Frankl (Man's Search for Meaning, 1946), he argued that self-transcendence, not self-actualisation, should be the aim of life.", "d": "Gordon Allport is known for trait theory (1937), the functional autonomy of motives and the proprium, not for self-actualisation."}}, {"q": "A culturally determined rule that modifies expression of emotion in a particular situation is called as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a culturally determined rule that modifies the expression of emotion in a particular situation is a display rule. Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1969) coined the term. Their research suggested that a few basic facial expressions (happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust) are universal, so cultures differ mainly in rules about when, where and to whom these expressions may be shown. A display rule can tell a person to intensify, weaken, neutralise or mask a feeling with another expression. In the classic study, Wallace Friesen (1972) filmed Japanese and American students watching stressful film clips. Alone, both groups showed the same expressions of disgust and fear; when an experimenter sat with them, the Japanese students more often masked negative expressions with smiles. Display rules are learned in childhood; by school age children know to hide disappointment over an unwanted gift. Example: at her grandmother's funeral Meera wants to laugh at a cousin's joke but keeps a solemn face, and at a wedding she smiles although she is exhausted; both are display rules at work. Option b is right because display rule is the technical name for a cultural norm that changes how emotion is shown. Exam trap: feeling rules (Arlie Hochschild, 1979) sound similar but concern what one should feel, not what one may show; and \"cultural rule\" is a vague phrase, not the term.", "traps": {"a": "Cultural rule is an everyday phrase, not the technical term; the concept is indeed cultural, but Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen named it a display rule.", "c": "Situational rule picks up the word situation from the stem but is not a named concept in emotion research.", "d": "All of these fails because cultural rule and situational rule are not technical terms; only display rule, option b, names the concept."}}, {"q": "To test James-Lange's theory Hohmann collected data from the persons with:", "concept": "The answer is b: George Hohmann tested the James-Lange theory with people who had spinal cord damage. The James-Lange theory, proposed by William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), says that an emotion is our perception of bodily changes: we feel afraid because we tremble. If that is true, people who receive less feedback from the body should feel weaker emotions. George Hohmann (1966) interviewed 25 male veterans with spinal cord injuries at different levels. The higher the lesion on the spinal cord, the less bodily feedback reaches the brain, and the more the men reported that their fear, anger and sexual feelings had lost intensity; one said his anger \"just doesn't have the heat to it that it used to\". This supported James and Lange. Example: Arjun, injured high in the neck in a road accident, says he still knows when something is unfair but no longer feels the surge of anger in his chest. Option b is right because Hohmann's participants were people with spinal cord damage. Exam trap: Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the same theory with animals, citing Charles Sherrington's dogs whose spinal cords had been cut and his own cats whose sympathetic nerves had been removed, which still showed emotional behaviour; students mix up the two lines of evidence. Note too that a later study by Kathleen Chwalisz, Ed Diener and Dennis Gallagher (1988) found people with spinal cord injuries reporting emotions about as intense as anyone else's.", "traps": {"a": "Brain damage studies address the emotional brain itself (for example amygdala lesions and fear), not the bodily feedback the James-Lange theory depends on; George Hohmann studied spinal cord injury.", "c": "Occipital damage affects vision, since the occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex, so it says nothing about feedback from the body to the brain.", "d": "None of these fails because spinal cord damage, option b, is exactly what Hohmann's participants had."}}, {"q": "The study of reaction time is called as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the study of reaction time as a window on mental processes is called mental chronometry. Mental chronometry measures how long mental operations take, using reaction time (RT), the interval between a stimulus and the response to it. It rests on the idea that mental processes take time and that each extra stage adds measurable time. Franciscus Donders (1868) founded it with the subtraction method: he subtracted simple RT (respond as soon as any light appears) from the RT of a task with an added stage, such as identifying the stimulus or choosing between responses, and treated the difference as the time that stage needs. Saul Sternberg (1966) timed memory scanning, showing that RT rose by about 38 milliseconds for each extra item held in memory, and later (1969) proposed the additive factors method. Michael Posner (1978) gathered such work in Chronometric Explorations of Mind. Example: in a lab, Meera presses a button as soon as a light flashes, then must press only for red lights and ignore green ones; she is slower the second time, and the extra time estimates how long identifying the colour takes. Option a is right because mental chronometry is the established term for using reaction time to study the mind. Exam trap: \"mental chronology\" looks almost the same, but chronology means the order of past events, not the timing of mental processes; \"reaction time studies\" is a description, not the name of the field.", "traps": {"b": "Mental chronology is a near-miss; chronology means the ordering of past events in time, whereas the timing of mental operations is chronometry.", "c": "Reaction time studies simply describes the activity; the established name of the field, since Franciscus Donders (1868), is mental chronometry.", "d": "None of these fails because mental chronometry, option a, is the correct term."}}, {"q": "A supporting cell found in the organ of Corti is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Deiters' cell is a supporting cell found in the organ of Corti. The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane inside the cochlea of the inner ear, and it contains two kinds of cells. The receptor cells are the hair cells: about 3,500 inner hair cells in a single row, which send almost all the signals to the auditory nerve, and about 12,000 outer hair cells in three rows, which amplify the vibration. Around them are supporting cells that hold the structure in shape. Deiters' cells, also called outer phalangeal cells and named after the German anatomist Otto Deiters (1834 to 1863), sit below the outer hair cells and cradle their bases, sending thin processes up to the surface between them. Other supporting cells include the pillar cells, which form the tunnel of Corti, and the Hensen's and Claudius cells at the outer edge. When sound moves the basilar membrane, the stereocilia of the hair cells bend against the tectorial membrane, ion channels open and the hair cell signals the nerve; the supporting cells keep the hair cells in the right position for this. Option a is right because Deiters' cells support the outer hair cells rather than detecting sound themselves. Exam trap: hair cells are the best-known cells of the organ of Corti, so students pick b, but hair cells are the sensory receptors that transduce sound, not supporting cells.", "traps": {"b": "Hair cells (inner and outer) are the sensory receptor cells of the organ of Corti that turn vibration into nerve signals; they are what the supporting cells hold, not supporting cells themselves.", "c": "Auditory cell is not a specific cell type; it is a vague label that could mean any cell in the hearing pathway.", "d": "Both (B) and (C) fails because hair cells are receptors and auditory cell is not a real cell type, so neither is a supporting cell."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is involved in pitch perception?", "concept": "The answer is c: both the place code and the rate code are involved in pitch perception. Pitch is the perceived highness or lowness of a sound, set mainly by its frequency (humans hear about 20 to 20,000 Hz). The place code comes from the place theory of Hermann von Helmholtz (1863): different frequencies make different places on the basilar membrane vibrate most. Georg von Bekesy confirmed this with his travelling wave (Nobel Prize 1961): high frequencies peak near the narrow, stiff base of the cochlea and low frequencies near the wide, floppy apex, so the brain reads pitch from which hair cells are most active. The rate (temporal or frequency) code comes from the frequency theory of William Rutherford (1886): auditory nerve fibres fire in time with the sound wave, so firing rate signals pitch. A single neuron cannot fire more than about 1,000 times a second, so Ernest Wever and Charles Bray (1930) proposed the volley principle: groups of fibres take turns and together keep pace with higher frequencies. The modern view combines the two: timing (phase locking) carries pitch for low frequencies, up to about 4,000 to 5,000 Hz, and place carries it for high frequencies. Example: when Riya hears a deep tabla stroke, the timing of nerve firing tells her its pitch; for a very high whistle above 5,000 Hz, the place of peak vibration does the work. Option c is right because both codes contribute. Exam trap: choosing only a or b treats the two theories as rivals; and the frequency theory is William Rutherford's, not the physicist Ernest Rutherford's.", "traps": {"a": "The place code (Hermann von Helmholtz's place theory, confirmed by Georg von Bekesy's travelling wave) explains high pitches well but not low ones; picking it alone leaves out the rate code.", "b": "The rate code (William Rutherford's frequency theory, extended by Ernest Wever and Charles Bray's volley principle) explains low pitches but fails above about 4,000 to 5,000 Hz; alone it leaves out the place code.", "d": "None of these fails because both listed codes are involved, so option c is right."}}, {"q": "A G protein that plays a vital role in transduction of sweetness and bitterness is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: gustducin is the G protein that plays a vital role in transducing sweet and bitter tastes. Taste cells work in two broad ways. Salty and sour tastes mainly use ion channels: sodium or hydrogen ions enter the taste cell directly. Sweet, bitter and umami tastes use receptors coupled to G proteins: the taste molecule binds a receptor on the cell surface, the receptor activates a G protein inside the cell, and a chain of second messengers ends in the release of transmitter onto the taste nerve. Susan McLaughlin, Peter McKinnon and Robert Margolskee (1992) discovered this taste-cell G protein and called it gustducin, because its alpha subunit closely resembles transducin, the G protein of the rods and cones of the retina (gust, from taste, plus transducin). Mice bred without gustducin lose much of their sensitivity to bitter and sweet substances, which shows how vital it is. Example: when Arjun sips bitter karela juice, the bitter molecules bind receptors that activate gustducin in his taste cells, the taste nerve fires, and he winces. Option a is right because gustducin is the named G protein of sweet and bitter transduction. Exam trap: umami sits in the same chapter and is also transduced through G protein receptors, but umami is a taste quality (the savoury taste of glutamate, named by Kikunae Ikeda in 1908), not a protein.", "traps": {"b": "Amyloid (beta-amyloid) is the protein fragment that clumps into the plaques of Alzheimer's disease; it has nothing to do with taste transduction.", "c": "Umami is the fifth basic taste, the savoury taste of glutamate named by Kikunae Ikeda (1908); it is a taste quality that uses G protein receptors, not a G protein itself.", "d": "None of these fails because gustducin, option a, is exactly the protein described."}}, {"q": "A step by step procedure involved in figuring out the correct answer to any problem is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: an algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that leads to the correct answer to a problem. Problem solving means moving from an initial state to a goal state, and psychologists separate two broad kinds of strategy. An algorithm is a fixed set of rules which, if followed exactly, always produces the solution: long division, a recipe followed to the letter, or trying every possible code on a padlock. Its weakness is time; when there are many possibilities an algorithm can be very slow. A heuristic is a rule of thumb that is quick and usually works but gives no guarantee. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon (1972), who modelled human problem solving with their General Problem Solver program, described means-end analysis, a heuristic in which you find the biggest difference between where you are and the goal and set a subgoal to reduce it. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) showed how judgement heuristics such as availability, representativeness and anchoring lead to systematic errors. Example: Kabir has forgotten the three-digit code of his bicycle lock. Trying 000, 001, 002 and so on up to 999 is an algorithm: slow but certain. Starting with the digits of his birth year is a heuristic: fast, but it may fail. Option a is right because only an algorithm is defined as a step-by-step procedure that guarantees the answer. Exam trap: heuristics and means-end analysis are also problem-solving strategies, so \"All of these\" tempts, but both trade the guarantee for speed.", "traps": {"b": "Heuristics are rules of thumb (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1974, studied availability and representativeness) that are fast but not guaranteed to give the correct answer, so they are not step-by-step guaranteed procedures.", "c": "Means-end analysis (Allen Newell and Herbert Simon) is a heuristic that sets subgoals to reduce the gap between the current state and the goal; it often works but does not guarantee a solution.", "d": "All of these fails because heuristics and means-end analysis give no guarantee; only an algorithm, option a, fits the definition."}}, {"q": "The unit of measurement in statistics is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the standard deviation is the unit of measurement in statistics. The standard deviation (SD, sigma) is the square root of the average squared deviation of scores from the mean; it tells you the typical distance of a score from the mean, in the original units. Karl Pearson (1894) gave it its name. Because it is a stable, standard distance, statisticians use it as a measuring stick for deviations. A z score expresses any deviation in SD units: z = (X minus mean) / SD, so a z of 2 means two SDs above the mean whatever the test. The normal curve table is also written in SD units: about 68 per cent of cases lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD. Example: Riya scores 70 on one test with mean 60 and SD 5, and 70 on another with mean 60 and SD 20. In SD units the first score is 2 SDs above the mean and the second only 0.5, so the first is the more impressive. Option d is right because only the SD works as a unit for measuring distance from the average. Exam trap: mean, median and mode are measures of central tendency; each is a single point in the distribution, the place you measure from, not the unit you measure with. Key note: the stem is loosely worded; it means the unit in which deviations are measured (psychology statistics texts such as Henry Garrett's Statistics in Psychology and Education measure distances along the normal curve in sigma units), and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The mean (arithmetic average) is a measure of central tendency, the point from which deviations are measured, not the unit in which they are measured.", "b": "The median is the middle score (the 50th percentile), a measure of central tendency and a single point, not a unit of measurement.", "c": "The mode is the most frequent score, a measure of central tendency that marks one point in the distribution, not a unit."}}, {"q": "A researcher wants to group people into low, average and high categories, the most suitable method to do this is:", "concept": "The answer is a: quartile deviation is the most suitable of these methods for grouping people into low, average and high categories. Quartiles are the three points that divide a ranked distribution into four equal parts. Q1, the 25th percentile, has a quarter of the scores below it; Q2 is the median; Q3, the 75th percentile, has three quarters below it. People below Q1 can be called low, those between Q1 and Q3 (the middle 50 per cent) average, and those above Q3 high. The quartile deviation, or semi-interquartile range, is built on exactly these points: Q = (Q3 minus Q1) / 2. Because it ignores the extreme quarters, it resists outliers and is the natural partner of the median. Example: a counsellor gives a stress scale to 200 students and finds Q1 is 32 and Q3 is 58. Meera, at 25, falls in the low group; Arjun, at 45, is average; Kabir, at 66, is high. Option a is right because the quartile approach supplies the cut-off points that create the three groups. Exam trap: the mean is a single point, so it can split a group into two (above and below average) but not into three; frequency is only a count of how many people got each score; and chi-square is a test of whether observed frequencies differ from expected ones. Key note: strictly it is the quartile points (Q1 and Q3) that do the grouping, and mean plus or minus 1 SD cut-offs are also used; among the four options only quartile deviation rests on those points, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Frequency is just the number of times each score or category occurs; it describes how scores are spread but supplies no cut-off points for low, average and high.", "c": "The mean is a single central point, so on its own it can only split people into above and below average; it cannot define three groups.", "d": "The chi-square test (Karl Pearson, 1900) checks whether observed frequencies differ from expected frequencies; it is a significance test, not a way of forming groups."}}, {"q": "The normal probability curve is not:", "concept": "The answer is c: the normal probability curve is not leptokurtic. The normal curve is the symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) as the limit of the binomial distribution and used by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) for errors in astronomical measurement. Its properties: it is bell-shaped and unimodal; it is symmetrical, so the mean, median and mode coincide at the centre; it is asymptotic to the baseline, meaning the tails come ever closer to the x-axis but never touch it; its total area is 1, so areas read as probabilities; and it is fully described by its mean and SD, with about 68, 95 and 99.7 per cent of cases within 1, 2 and 3 SD. Kurtosis describes how peaked a curve is, and how heavy its tails are, compared with the normal. The normal curve is the reference point: mesokurtic, with a moment kurtosis of 3 (excess kurtosis 0). A leptokurtic curve is more peaked with heavier tails (kurtosis above 3); a platykurtic curve is flatter (below 3). Example: Kabir plots the ratings of 300 students on a scale where almost everyone marks close to the average and only a few give extreme answers; that tall, thin curve with long tails is leptokurtic, not normal. Option c is right because the normal curve is mesokurtic by definition, so it cannot be leptokurtic. Exam trap: this is a \"not\" question; a, b and d are all true properties, and examiners count on students skimming past \"not\", or confusing lepto (slender, peaked) with meso (middle).", "traps": {"a": "Being asymptotic to the baseline (the tails approach the x-axis but never touch it) is a true property of the normal curve, so it cannot answer a question about what the curve is not.", "b": "Bell shape is the defining look of the normal curve (symmetrical and unimodal), so this is something the curve is.", "d": "Mesokurtic (kurtosis 3, excess kurtosis 0) is exactly what the normal curve is; it is the standard against which leptokurtic and platykurtic curves are judged."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is incorrect?", "concept": "The answer is b: the incorrect statement is that statistical significance matters more than managerial significance. Statistical significance tells you only that a result is unlikely to be due to chance alone (p below .05, say). It depends heavily on sample size: with thousands of respondents even a tiny difference becomes significant. Managerial (practical) significance asks whether the difference is big enough to matter for a decision. Business research texts, such as Donald Cooper and Pamela Schindler's Business Research Methods, stress that a statistically significant but trivial result should not drive decisions, so managerial significance matters more. Psychology makes the same point: Jacob Cohen (1994), in \"The earth is round (p < .05)\", attacked blind reliance on p values and urged effect sizes, and Neil Jacobson and Paula Truax (1991) proposed testing clinical significance as reliable change that moves a client into the normal range of functioning. Statements C and D are plainly true: a raw score converts to a z score by z = (X minus mean) / SD, and z scores are standard scores with mean 0 and SD 1. Example: a survey of 20,000 shoppers finds that they prefer the new packaging designed by Riya's team by 0.2 per cent, with p below .01. The result is significant, but no manager would spend crores on a redesign for such a gain. Option b is right because it reverses the accepted priority. Exam trap: in an \"incorrect statement\" item, a and b are mirror images, so one of them must be false; students who equate \"significant\" with \"important\" choose a. Key note: which matters more is a judgement, but the research texts this item follows state A, so B is the incorrect statement and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "This is the correct position in business research texts (Donald Cooper and Pamela Schindler; Naresh Malhotra): a result must be large enough to matter for decisions, not just statistically significant, so it is not the incorrect statement.", "c": "A raw score can always be converted into a z score with z = (X minus mean) / SD, so this statement is true.", "d": "Z scores are standard scores, with a mean of 0 and an SD of 1, so this statement is true."}}, {"q": "People who are easily upset fall high on:", "concept": "The answer is c: people who are easily upset score high on neuroticism. Neuroticism is the personality dimension of emotional instability: a tendency to feel anxiety, worry, moodiness, irritability and sadness, and to be thrown off balance by small stresses. Its opposite pole is emotional stability (calm, even-tempered, resilient). Hans Eysenck (1947) made it one of his two original superfactors, alongside extraversion, and later added psychoticism; his questionnaires (the MPI, EPI and EPQ) all score it. Eysenck linked high neuroticism to a reactive limbic system, the 'visceral brain', which sets off strong emotional arousal quickly and keeps it going. Neuroticism also survives as one of the Big Five factors of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985), whose NEO inventories break it into facets such as anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Example: Riya is shaken for days by a mild remark from her teacher, worries before every test and cries when plans change; she would score high on neuroticism, whether she is outgoing or shy. Option c is right because 'easily upset' describes emotional instability, which is exactly what the neuroticism dimension measures. Exam trap: students link being upset with being quiet and withdrawn and pick introversion. But introversion and extraversion concern sociability and arousal, and they are independent of neuroticism: Eysenck crossed the two dimensions to get four types, so there are stable and unstable introverts and stable and unstable extraverts.", "traps": {"a": "Introversion is the quiet, reserved pole of Eysenck's extraversion dimension, linked to higher cortical arousal; it describes sociability, not emotional stability, so an introvert can be very calm.", "b": "Extraversion is the sociable, outgoing, sensation-seeking pole of the same dimension; like introversion it says nothing about how easily a person is upset.", "d": "None of these does not fit, because neuroticism (option c) is the dimension defined by being easily upset, anxious and moody."}}, {"q": "The different forms of a gene are called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the different forms of a gene are called alleles. A gene is a stretch of DNA at a fixed place (locus) on a chromosome that usually carries the code for a protein. Many genes come in more than one version, and each version is an allele. Because we receive one chromosome of each pair from each parent, every person carries two alleles of each gene on the autosomes. Two identical alleles make a person homozygous for that gene; two different ones make the person heterozygous. A dominant allele shows its effect even when only one copy is present, while a recessive allele shows only when both copies are recessive. Gregor Mendel (1866) worked this out in pea plants: his 'factors' for purple and white flowers were what we now call two alleles of one gene. The word is short for 'allelomorph' (from the Greek for 'one another' and 'form'), coined by William Bateson and Edith Saunders (1902). The alleles a person carries make up the genotype; the observable result is the phenotype. Example: Meera carries an A allele and an O allele of the ABO blood group gene; A is dominant over O, so her blood group is A, yet she can pass the O allele to her child. Option b is right because alleles are, by definition, alternative forms of the same gene at the same locus. Exam trap: traits and characters are what genes help to produce, the visible features of the phenotype, not the gene versions themselves; students who read 'different forms' as 'different features' fall for them.", "traps": {"a": "Traits are observable characteristics (height, flower colour, blood group) that result from genes acting with the environment; they belong to the phenotype, not to the alternative versions of a gene.", "c": "Characters is Mendel's older word for the visible features he tracked, such as seed shape or flower colour; a character is what an allele influences, not the allele itself.", "d": "All of these fails because traits and characters are features of the phenotype; only alleles (option b) are the different forms of a gene."}}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis places too much emphasis on:", "concept": "The answer is a: psychoanalysis is criticised for placing too much emphasis on the unconscious mind. Sigmund Freud (1900) divided the mind in his topographic model into three levels: the conscious (what you are aware of right now), the preconscious (memories and facts you can bring to awareness easily, like your phone number) and the unconscious (wishes, memories and conflicts kept out of awareness, mostly by repression, which still drive behaviour). For Freud the unconscious was the largest and most important part, the submerged bulk of the iceberg: dreams, slips of the tongue, neurotic symptoms and even career choices were traced to hidden sexual and aggressive wishes formed in early childhood. Critics argued this went too far. Karl Popper (1963) said the theory could explain any outcome and so could never be shown to be false. Behaviourists said unconscious forces cannot be observed, and humanistic and cognitive psychologists said Freud neglected conscious choice, reasoning and present goals. Example: Arjun forgets his dentist appointment because he was busy, but a strict Freudian reads it as an unconscious wish to avoid pain, a reading Arjun can neither confirm nor refute. Option a is right because the stock criticism of psychoanalysis is its over-reliance on unconscious, unobservable causes (along with sexuality and early childhood). Exam trap: the preconscious sounds hidden too, but Freud gave it only a minor bridging role; and the conscious mind is what his critics wanted more weight placed on, the reverse of his emphasis.", "traps": {"b": "The conscious mind, our present awareness, was the smallest part of Freud's model; ego psychologists, humanists and cognitive therapists stress it, so psychoanalysis is criticised for neglecting it, not for overstressing it.", "c": "The preconscious holds material that can be recalled easily (what you ate yesterday); Freud treated it as a passage between the conscious and the unconscious and gave it little weight, so it is not what critics complain about.", "d": "All of these fails because psychoanalysis gave the conscious and preconscious levels a small role; only the unconscious (option a) is over-emphasised."}}, {"q": "Peak experiences are central to:", "concept": "The answer is a: peak experiences are central to Abraham Maslow's theory. Abraham Maslow (1962) described peak experiences as brief, intense moments of joy, wonder, awe and unity, when a person feels whole, loses track of time and self, and sees the world as good and meaningful. He met them first in his study of self-actualisers, the healthy, creative people at the top of his hierarchy of needs, who reported them often; he later argued that almost everyone has them at some time. He wrote about them in Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) and Religions, Values, and Peak-Experiences (1964), and linked them to 'being cognition' (B-cognition), seeing things as they are rather than through one's needs. Peak experiences can come from love, music, nature, childbirth, creative work or prayer, and are often followed by a lasting change in outlook. Example: Kabir finishes a long trek at sunrise in the Himalayas and, for a few minutes, feels complete awe and oneness with everything around him; Maslow would call this a peak experience. Option a is right because the concept was named and developed by Maslow as part of his humanistic theory of self-actualisation. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1961) and Viktor Frankl (1946) are also humanistic or existential names linked to growth and meaning, so students mix them up; but Rogers's signature ideas are the fully functioning person and unconditional positive regard, and Frankl's is the will to meaning.", "traps": {"b": "Carl Rogers's person-centred theory centres on the self-concept, congruence, conditions of worth and the fully functioning person; peak experiences are not one of his core terms.", "c": "Viktor Frankl's logotherapy centres on the will to meaning and self-transcendence, found through work, love and the attitude taken to suffering; peak experiences belong to Maslow, not Frankl.", "d": "None of these fails because Maslow's theory (option a) is exactly where peak experiences are central."}}, {"q": "The book The Interpretation of Dreams was written by:", "concept": "The answer is b: The Interpretation of Dreams was written by Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1900) wrote Die Traumdeutung; it appeared in November 1899, though the title page bears the date 1900. He argued that every dream is the disguised fulfilment of a wish, often a forbidden sexual or aggressive one. What we remember on waking is the manifest content; the hidden meaning behind it is the latent content. The dream work turns latent into manifest content through condensation (several ideas fused into one image), displacement (feeling shifted from an important target to a trivial one), symbolisation, and secondary revision (the mind stitches the result into a story). Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road to a knowledge of the unconscious activities of the mind', and used his own dreams, such as the dream of Irma's injection, as worked examples. Example: Riya dreams that she misses a train; a Freudian would ask what wish the dream disguises, perhaps a wish to escape an exam she dreads. Option b is right because the book is Freud's founding work on dreams and the unconscious. Exam trap: Anna O sounds like a Freud author because her case is famous, but she was Bertha Pappenheim, the patient of Josef Breuer (1895) in Studies on Hysteria, who called the treatment 'the talking cure'; she wrote no book on dreams. Alfred Adler, another early analyst, is the other decoy.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1912), who broke with Freud in 1911 and founded individual psychology, wrote The Neurotic Constitution (1912) and Understanding Human Nature (1927), not The Interpretation of Dreams.", "c": "Smith is a filler name with no link to this book; no psychologist called Smith wrote The Interpretation of Dreams.", "d": "Anna O was Bertha Pappenheim, Josef Breuer's hysteria patient in Studies on Hysteria (1895), who called the method 'the talking cure'; she was a case, not the author."}}, {"q": "Past experiences are to Freud as fictional finalism is to:", "concept": "The answer is a: fictional finalism is Alfred Adler's idea. Alfred Adler (1912), the founder of individual psychology, argued that people are pulled forward by goals rather than pushed by the past. Fictional finalism is the idea that behaviour is guided by imagined final goals: beliefs about the future that cannot be tested and may not be true, yet work as if they were, such as 'if I become rich I will be safe' or 'honest people are always rewarded'. Adler took the 'as if' idea from Hans Vaihinger (1911), whose The Philosophy of 'As If' held that people live by useful fictions. The final goal, usually a fiction of superiority or perfection born from childhood feelings of inferiority, organises a person's whole style of life. Freud's view is causal and deterministic: present behaviour is explained by past experiences, especially early childhood conflicts. Adler's view is teleological: behaviour is explained by where the person is heading. That is the analogy in the stem: past experiences are to Freud as imagined future goals are to Adler. Example: Kabir, small and teased as a child, lives by the private fiction that 'only toppers are respected', and that imagined goal, not the teasing itself, shapes his daily choices. Option a is right because fictional finalism is a core concept of Adler's individual psychology. Exam trap: Gordon Allport's (1937) functional autonomy also cuts motives loose from the past, so Allport tempts students; but it describes present motives running on their own, not guidance by imagined future goals.", "traps": {"b": "Ringle is not a known personality theorist; the name is a filler (or misprint) with no link to fictional finalism.", "c": "Raymond Cattell built a factor-analytic trait theory (surface and source traits, ergs and sentiments, the 16PF); it explains behaviour by measured traits, not by imagined future goals.", "d": "Gordon Allport's functional autonomy says adult motives become independent of their origins and run in the present; it is not about fictional goals pulling a person forward."}}, {"q": "SEMS are socially acquired and can satisfy:", "concept": "The answer is c: sems, the socially acquired dynamic traits, can satisfy several ergs at a time. Raymond Cattell (1957) divided the dynamic (motivational) traits into two kinds. Ergs are innate, biologically based drives, such as hunger, sex, fear (security), curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion; textbooks list about ten or eleven of them. Metanergs are learned through culture and experience. They include sentiments, large organised attitude systems centred on an important object such as career, family, religion or the self, and attitudes, specific interests in a particular course of action. Cattell himself later called sentiments 'sems', short for socially shaped ergic manifolds. Cattell linked them in a chain he called subsidiation: an attitude serves a sentiment, and a sentiment serves ergs. The whole web of links is the dynamic lattice, and its central feature is that one sentiment feeds many drives at once, while one erg can be fed by many sentiments. Example: Arjun's sentiment for his medical career satisfies self-assertion (status), security (a stable income), gregariousness (working with colleagues) and curiosity (learning about the body), all through the same set of attitudes, such as wanting to study tonight. Option c is right because, in the dynamic lattice, a learned sentiment normally serves several ergs at the same time. Exam trap: the stem looks as if it wants a fixed number, so 'one' or 'two' tempt students; but Cattell's point is that sentiments are general-purpose channels, with no fixed ratio of one sentiment to one or two drives.", "traps": {"a": "One erg at a time would make each sentiment a single-purpose channel; Cattell's dynamic lattice shows one sentiment (career, family) serving several innate drives at once.", "b": "Two ergs at a time is an arbitrary limit; Cattell set no such number, and a sentiment usually serves several ergs, as the career example shows.", "d": "None of these fails because several ergs at a time (option c) is exactly what Cattell's subsidiation chain describes."}}, {"q": "Allport was interested in which of the following constructs?", "concept": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport worked on all three constructs, traits, rumour and prejudice. Gordon Allport (1937) is best known as a founder of trait psychology. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation he treated traits as real neuropsychic structures and sorted them into cardinal (one ruling disposition), central (the handful that describe a person) and secondary (narrow preferences) traits; with Henry Odbert (1936) he listed 17,953 English trait words from the dictionary, the start of the lexical approach. During the Second World War he studied rumour. The Psychology of Rumor (1947), written with Leo Postman, gave a basic law: rumour strength varies with the importance of the topic times its ambiguity (R ~ i x a). Their serial reproduction studies showed that retold stories lose detail (levelling), keep a few vivid points (sharpening) and are reshaped to fit the teller's expectations (assimilation). In The Nature of Prejudice (1954) he defined prejudice as an antipathy based on a faulty and inflexible generalisation, and proposed the contact hypothesis: contact between groups reduces prejudice under equal status, common goals, cooperation and support from authorities. Example: a false story that exams are postponed races through Meera's hostel because the topic matters to everyone and the facts are unclear, just as Allport's rumour law predicts. Option d is right because each of the three constructs is a major Allport topic. Exam trap: students know Allport only as a trait theorist and pick 'Traits'; rumour and prejudice are listed precisely to catch that narrow reading.", "traps": {"a": "Traits were Allport's main subject, but choosing traits alone leaves out his rumour studies (1947, with Leo Postman) and his work on prejudice (1954).", "b": "Rumour was a real Allport topic, but choosing it alone misses his trait theory and his work on prejudice.", "c": "Prejudice was a real Allport topic, but choosing it alone misses his trait theory and his rumour research."}}, {"q": "Choose the incorrect statement:", "concept": "The answer is b: the incorrect statement is that posttraumatic growth is harmful. Posttraumatic growth is the positive psychological change some people report after struggling with a highly stressful or traumatic event. Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun developed the idea in the mid-1990s and built the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (1996), which measures five domains: new possibilities, relating to others, personal strength, spiritual change and appreciation of life. Growth does not mean the trauma was good or that distress is absent; it comes from the struggle to rebuild one's beliefs afterwards, and it can sit alongside pain. It is therefore a positive construct, so statement A is true. Posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is a different outcome, and its intrusion symptoms (DSM-5 criterion B) include flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again; so statement D is true. Example: Meera loses her home in a flood; months later she still has bad dreams, but she also says she values her family more and has found a strength she did not know she had. Option b is right because calling posttraumatic growth harmful contradicts its definition as beneficial change after trauma. Exam trap: the word 'posttraumatic' makes students think of damage and PTSD, so 'harmful' sounds plausible; but growth is the positive side of the same experience. Key note: statement C is also shaky, since trauma is an extreme stressor and DSM-5 places PTSD under trauma- and stressor-related disorders, so stress and trauma overlap rather than being entirely different. But statement B contradicts the definition of posttraumatic growth outright, so b, the answer used here, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Statement A is true: posttraumatic growth is defined as positive change (new possibilities, personal strength, appreciation of life), so it is a positive construct, not the incorrect statement.", "c": "Statement C is loosely worded, since trauma is an extreme form of stress, but DSM-5 does treat them as distinct (a trauma needs exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence); it is not as plainly false as B.", "d": "Statement D is true: flashbacks are a dissociative intrusion symptom of PTSD under DSM-5 criterion B."}}, {"q": "The people with OCD perform:", "concept": "The answer is b: people with OCD have been found to perform better on procedural memory tasks. Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits, such as riding a bicycle or typing, shown in performance rather than conscious recall. It depends heavily on the basal ganglia, especially the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen), working in loops with the frontal cortex. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) involves intrusive thoughts (obsessions) and repetitive acts done to reduce anxiety (compulsions), and brain imaging shows over-activity in a loop linking the orbitofrontal cortex, caudate and thalamus. Because this over-active fronto-striatal circuit also serves habits, researchers asked whether OCD might sharpen procedural learning. Robert Roth and colleagues (2004) compared 27 adults with OCD with 29 healthy controls: the OCD group beat controls on early trials of the pursuit rotor, a motor skill task, while their verbal declarative memory was normal. Many studies, by contrast, find people with OCD weaker on explicit memory, especially for non-verbal material, and low in confidence about their memory, which helps explain repeated checking. Example: Kabir, who has OCD, keeps doubting whether he locked the door, yet picks up a new hand-eye tracking task quickly. Option b is right because the reported finding is an advantage on procedural tasks, linked to striatal over-activation. Exam trap: since OCD is a disorder, students assume every memory system is impaired and pick 'badly'; the deficits lie mainly in explicit memory and memory confidence. Key note: the evidence is mixed; Roth's advantage faded on later trials, a PET study by Scott Rauch and colleagues (1997) found normal sequence learning that recruited medial temporal rather than striatal areas, and some studies report implicit learning deficits. But procedural memory is the only option with a reported advantage, so b, the answer used here, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Badly on procedural tasks fits the popular idea that OCD harms all memory, but the reported deficits are mainly in explicit (especially non-verbal) memory and memory confidence; procedural learning has been found intact or even enhanced.", "c": "Average performance matches some studies that found normal sequence learning, but the specific finding this item reflects (Robert Roth and colleagues, 2004) is better-than-control procedural performance.", "d": "None of these fails because a procedural advantage (option b) has been reported, linked to over-activity in the striatum."}}, {"q": "The phobia of being out of cellular phone contact is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the phobia of being out of mobile phone contact is called nomophobia. Nomophobia is short for 'no mobile phone phobia': anxiety or distress when a person cannot use their phone because it is lost, the battery is flat or there is no network, or when they are simply out of contact. The word came from a 2008 survey commissioned by the UK Post Office and run by YouGov, which found that about half of British mobile users felt anxious in these situations. Typical signs are checking the phone constantly, keeping it switched on in bed, carrying a charger everywhere and feeling panic when it is out of reach. It is not an official DSM-5 diagnosis; researchers treat it as a modern, situation-bound fear linked to problematic smartphone use and measure it with questionnaires such as the Nomophobia Questionnaire. Example: Kabir's phone dies on a long bus ride; his heart races and he keeps reaching for his pocket, worried that he is missing messages. That is nomophobia. Option a is right because nomophobia is the established term for the fear of being without mobile phone contact. Exam trap: 'cellular phobia' copies a word from the stem and sounds right, but it is not a recognised term; examiners rely on students choosing the most literal-sounding option. The other two are real phobia names with unrelated objects.", "traps": {"b": "Cellular phobia is not a recognised term; it simply borrows 'cellular' from the stem. The accepted name is nomophobia.", "c": "Escalaphobia is the fear of escalators, a specific situational fear with no link to phones.", "d": "Emetophobia is the fear of vomiting (or of seeing others vomit), a specific phobia unrelated to phone contact."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "concept": "The answer is b: the correct statement is that the exact cause of panic disorder is unknown. DSM-5 diagnoses panic disorder when a person has recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (sudden surges of intense fear that peak within minutes, with a pounding heart, breathlessness, dizziness and fear of dying or losing control), followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks or a change in behaviour, such as avoiding places. No single cause has been found. Instead, several models each explain part of the picture: a genetic vulnerability (it runs in families); the cognitive model of David Clark (1986), in which people catastrophically misinterpret harmless bodily sensations (a fast heartbeat means a heart attack), so fear spirals into panic; the suffocation false alarm theory of Donald Klein (1993), in which an over-sensitive brain alarm misreads rising carbon dioxide as suffocation; and the triple vulnerability model of David Barlow (2002), which combines a general biological, a general psychological and a specific learned vulnerability. Example: Arjun has his first attack on a crowded metro; afterwards he notices every flutter in his chest and avoids the metro. His psychologist can describe how the cycle works but cannot name one exact cause. Option b is right because panic disorder is understood through several interacting biological and psychological models, not one known cause. Exam trap: statement C reverses the diagnosis, since DSM-5 actually requires unexpected attacks; and statement D reverses the age pattern, since onset is usually in late adolescence or early adulthood and panic disorder is uncommon in young children.", "traps": {"a": "Statement A is false: no single exact cause is known; genetic, biological (Donald Klein's suffocation false alarm) and cognitive (David Clark's catastrophic misinterpretation) factors all contribute.", "c": "Statement C is false: DSM-5 panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks, attacks that come out of the blue without an obvious trigger.", "d": "Statement D is false: panic disorder usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood and is rare in young children, so it is more common in adults."}}, {"q": "The catecholamine hypothesis explains the causes of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the catecholamine hypothesis explains the causes of depression. Catecholamines are a family of monoamine neurotransmitters made from the amino acid tyrosine: dopamine, noradrenaline (norepinephrine) and adrenaline (epinephrine). Joseph Schildkraut (1965) proposed the catecholamine hypothesis of affective disorders: depression is linked to a functional deficiency of catecholamines, especially noradrenaline, at important brain synapses, and mania to an excess. William Bunney and John Davis (1965) put forward the same noradrenaline idea in the same year. The evidence came from drugs. Reserpine, a blood pressure medicine that depletes monoamines from nerve endings, made some patients depressed, whereas the first antidepressants raised monoamine levels: monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors block their breakdown and tricyclics block their reuptake. A sister idea, the serotonin (indoleamine) hypothesis, came from Alec Coppen (1967); together they form the monoamine hypothesis. Later research showed the story is too simple: antidepressants raise transmitter levels within hours, yet mood takes weeks to lift, so slower receptor changes and neuroplasticity are now thought to matter. Example: Meera's psychiatrist explains that her antidepressant boosts noradrenaline and serotonin at her synapses, an explanation that comes straight from the catecholamine and serotonin hypotheses. Option d is right because the hypothesis was framed to explain depression (and mania), the affective disorders. Exam trap: noradrenaline and adrenaline are also part of the stress and fight-or-flight response, so 'stress' and 'anxiety' tempt students; but the catecholamine hypothesis is a theory of mood disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Anxiety is explained by other biological models, such as reduced GABA activity (the target of benzodiazepines) and an over-reactive amygdala; the catecholamine hypothesis was framed for mood disorders.", "b": "Stress involves catecholamines (adrenaline and noradrenaline released in the fight-or-flight response), but stress is a response, not the disorder the hypothesis set out to explain.", "c": "Generalised anxiety disorder is linked mainly to GABA and serotonin systems and to cognitive models of worry; the catecholamine hypothesis concerns depression and mania."}}, {"q": "The full form of SAD in context of psychopathology is:", "concept": "The answer is a: SAD stands for seasonal affective disorder. Seasonal affective disorder is depression that comes and goes with the seasons. In the usual winter pattern, low mood, low energy, oversleeping, craving for carbohydrates and weight gain begin in autumn or winter and lift in spring and summer, year after year. Norman Rosenthal and colleagues (1984) described and named it at the US National Institute of Mental Health. DSM-5 does not list SAD as a separate disorder; it is coded as recurrent major depressive disorder or bipolar disorder 'with seasonal pattern', a specifier that needs a regular link between episodes and a particular time of year, with full remission at another time, over at least two years. The leading explanation is reduced daylight, which shifts circadian rhythms and the timing of melatonin release, along with changes in serotonin. The main treatment is bright light therapy: sitting each day in front of a light box of about 10,000 lux, usually in the morning. Example: every November Riya, who studies in a northern city with short grey days, becomes tired and withdrawn and sleeps late; by March she feels like herself again. Option a is right because 'seasonal affective disorder' is the established name for this recurrent seasonal depression. Exam trap: the other options mix real words ('somatic', 'affective', 'alarm') into plausible acronyms, and 'somatic' points students towards somatic symptom disorders, which have nothing to do with the seasons.", "traps": {"b": "Somatic affective disorder is not a diagnosis; 'somatic' belongs to somatic symptom disorder, where bodily complaints, not seasonal mood, are the problem.", "c": "Seasonal alarm disorder is an invented expansion; 'alarm' recalls panic attacks or Hans Selye's (1936) alarm stage of stress, not seasonal depression.", "d": "Somatic affective disorganization is an invented phrase with no place in DSM-5 or ICD."}}, {"q": "The DSM-5 organizes personality disorders into:", "concept": "The answer is b: DSM-5 organises personality disorders into 3 clusters. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 (2013) lists 10 specific personality disorders and groups them by shared features into three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A handy memory aid is 'weird, wild and worried' for A, B and C. The clusters are a teaching and research convenience with limited validity, and many people meet criteria for disorders in more than one cluster. DSM-5 also offers, in Section III, an Alternative Model for Personality Disorders that rates impairment in personality functioning plus five pathological trait domains. Example: a clinician notes that Arjun is suspicious of everyone and keeps to himself, which points to Cluster A, while his cousin Kabir is anxious, clingy and afraid of disapproval, which points to Cluster C. Option b is right because the categorical DSM-5 system uses exactly three clusters, labelled A, B and C. Exam trap: 10 is the number of personality disorders, not the number of clusters, and students who remember '10' grab that option.", "traps": {"a": "One cluster would lump all ten disorders together; DSM-5 separates them into odd (A), dramatic (B) and anxious (C) groups.", "c": "There is no fourth cluster in DSM-5; patterns that fit none of the ten disorders are coded as other specified or unspecified personality disorder, or personality change due to another medical condition.", "d": "Ten is the number of specific personality disorders in DSM-5, not the number of clusters into which they are grouped."}}, {"q": "Which personality disorder is characterized by exaggerated self image?", "concept": "The answer is a: an exaggerated self-image is the hallmark of narcissistic personality disorder. Narcissistic personality disorder (NPD), one of DSM-5's Cluster B disorders, is a pervasive pattern of grandiosity (in fantasy or behaviour), need for admiration and lack of empathy, beginning by early adulthood. At least 5 of 9 criteria are needed: a grandiose sense of self-importance (exaggerating achievements and expecting to be seen as superior); fantasies of unlimited success, power or beauty; a belief that one is special and should mix only with high-status people; a need for excessive admiration; a sense of entitlement; exploiting others; lack of empathy; envy; and arrogant behaviour. The name comes from the Greek myth of Narcissus, who fell in love with his own reflection. Beneath the grandiosity self-esteem is often fragile, so criticism can trigger rage or shame. Example: Kabir tells everyone that his ordinary work project will change the industry, expects special treatment from his manager and cannot see why colleagues are hurt when he dismisses their work. Option a is right because a grandiose, inflated sense of self-importance, the exaggerated self-image of the stem, is the core feature of NPD. Exam trap: borderline personality disorder also has a 'self-image' criterion, but it is an unstable, shifting self-image (identity disturbance), not an exaggerated one; and histrionic patients seek attention through emotion and appearance, not through claims of superiority.", "traps": {"b": "Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) means excessive emotionality and attention seeking, shallow shifting emotions and use of physical appearance to draw attention; the aim is attention, not an inflated sense of superiority.", "c": "Borderline personality disorder involves an unstable self-image (identity disturbance), unstable relationships, impulsivity, fear of abandonment and self-harm; the self-image is shaky, not exaggerated.", "d": "Schizoid personality disorder (Cluster A) is detachment from social relationships with a restricted range of emotional expression; it involves no grandiosity."}}, {"q": "Desensitizing is:", "concept": "The answer is b: desensitisation is classical conditioning in action. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, treats phobias and anxiety by counterconditioning. In classical conditioning terms, the feared object has become a conditioned stimulus that triggers anxiety. Therapy pairs that same stimulus with a new response that cannot coexist with anxiety, deep relaxation; Wolpe called this reciprocal inhibition, since a person cannot be relaxed and anxious at once. There are three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of situations from least to most frightening, each rated in SUDS (subjective units of disturbance, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each step in turn, moving up only when the current step no longer causes anxiety. A forerunner was Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed a small boy called Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate food he liked. Example: Meera fears dogs; while relaxed she pictures a dog far away in a park, then a puppy across the room, and finally imagines patting a dog, staying calm at each image before moving on. Option b is right because the method works through new stimulus pairings (feared stimulus with relaxation), not through rewards or punishments. Exam trap: a therapy with graded steps sounds like operant shaping, so students pick 'operant' or 'both'; but desensitisation does not reinforce the client's behaviour, so it is a classical (respondent) technique.", "traps": {"a": "Operant conditioning (Edward Thorndike, 1898; B. F. Skinner, 1938) changes behaviour through its consequences; operant therapies are token economies, shaping and contingency management, not relaxation paired with feared stimuli.", "c": "Both is tempting because behaviour therapy as a whole uses both kinds of learning, but systematic desensitisation itself rests on classical counterconditioning, so the operant half does not apply.", "d": "Very harmful is false: systematic desensitisation is a gentle, graded and well-supported treatment for phobias, designed precisely to keep anxiety low."}}, {"q": "Cognitive therapy emphasizes on:", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive therapy emphasises what people think. Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck (1960s; set out fully in Cognitive Therapy and the Emotional Disorders, 1976), rests on the idea that it is not events themselves but the way we interpret them that shapes how we feel and act. Beck noticed that depressed patients had a stream of quick, negative automatic thoughts ('I always fail'), produced by deeper core beliefs or schemas ('I am worthless') and bent by systematic thinking errors such as overgeneralisation, catastrophising, all-or-nothing thinking and personalisation. In depression these form the cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world and the future. Therapist and client work as collaborators: they identify thoughts, test them against evidence (Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioural experiments) and replace them with more balanced ones. Albert Ellis (1955) built a parallel approach, first called rational therapy and now rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs using an ABC model. Example: Riya fails one test and thinks, 'I am stupid and will never pass'; her therapist helps her list the tests she has passed and rewrite the thought as 'I did badly on this one because I revised the wrong chapter'. Option a is right because cognitive therapy targets thoughts, beliefs and interpretations as the main source of distress. Exam trap: modern practice is usually cognitive-behavioural therapy, which works on both thoughts and actions, so 'Both' is a near miss; but the stem asks about cognitive therapy, whose defining emphasis is thinking.", "traps": {"b": "What people do is the focus of behaviour therapy (Joseph Wolpe, the Skinnerian operant therapists), which changes actions through conditioning and practice; cognitive therapy uses behavioural tasks mainly to test thoughts.", "c": "Both describes cognitive-behavioural therapy, which combines the two; cognitive therapy as such centres on thinking, so behaviour is not its defining emphasis.", "d": "What people observe points to Albert Bandura's (1977) observational learning and modelling, a social learning idea, not the focus of cognitive therapy."}}, {"q": "Which of the following belongs to the humanistic school?", "concept": "The answer is d: Sartre, Buber and Kierkegaard all belong to the humanistic (humanistic-existential) school. Humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, grew in the 1950s and 1960s around Abraham Maslow (1954) and Carl Rogers (1951). Its sister approach, existential psychology, drew on existentialist philosophers, and most textbooks treat the two as one humanistic-existential school because both stress freedom, choice, responsibility, subjective experience and the whole person. Soren Kierkegaard (1844), the Danish philosopher often called the father of existentialism, wrote in The Concept of Anxiety of anxiety as 'the dizziness of freedom', and stressed the individual's task of choosing who to become. Martin Buber (1923), in I and Thou, contrasted the I-Thou relationship (meeting another person as a whole, genuine being) with the I-It relationship (treating them as an object), an idea Rogers drew on for the therapeutic encounter. Jean-Paul Sartre (1943), author of Being and Nothingness, held that we are 'condemned to be free' and, in his famous slogan, that existence precedes essence: we make ourselves through our choices. Rollo May, Ernest Angel and Henri Ellenberger brought these ideas into American psychology in Existence (1958). Example: when Kabir's counsellor tells him that he, not his parents or fate, is responsible for choosing his path, she is drawing on Sartre and Kierkegaard. Option d is right because each of the three supplied core ideas to the humanistic-existential school. Exam trap: students know only Maslow and Rogers as humanists and so reject philosophers' names; examiners list them to test the existential roots. Key note: strictly, these three are existentialist philosophers rather than humanistic psychologists, but textbooks group the humanistic and existential approaches as one school, so d, the answer used here, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Jean-Paul Sartre is one of the existentialist sources of the school, but choosing him alone leaves out Buber and Kierkegaard, who belong equally.", "b": "Martin Buber's I-Thou idea is equally part of the school's roots, so choosing him alone leaves out Sartre and Kierkegaard.", "c": "Soren Kierkegaard, the father of existentialism, belongs too, but choosing him alone leaves out Sartre and Buber."}}, {"q": "Existential therapy focuses on the:", "concept": "The answer is d: existential therapy focuses on self-determination, free will and meaning. Existential therapy is not one technique but an approach that helps clients face the basic givens of human life. Irvin Yalom (1980), in Existential Psychotherapy, named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Freedom here means that we are free to choose and therefore responsible for our lives; self-determination and free will are two names for this theme, and dodging responsibility (blaming parents, fate or circumstances) is seen as a source of anxiety and stuckness. Rollo May (1969), in Love and Will, stressed freedom, will and the anxiety that comes with choice. Meaning is the theme of Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy, developed in the 1930s and tested by his concentration camp experience, holds that the will to meaning is the primary human motive, and that meaning can be found in work, in love and in the attitude one takes towards suffering. The therapist works through an honest, here-and-now relationship rather than set techniques, helping the client accept responsibility and choose authentically. Example: Arjun feels his life is empty and blames his parents for his engineering degree; his existential therapist helps him see that he still chooses what to do next and what will make his life meaningful. Option d is right because self-determination, free will and meaning are all central existential themes. Exam trap: 'self-determination' also recalls the self-determination theory of motivation of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), which makes some students doubt option a; in existential therapy the word simply means the freedom and responsibility to choose.", "traps": {"a": "Self-determination (the freedom and responsibility to choose) is one existential theme, but choosing it alone leaves out free will and meaning.", "b": "Free will is central, especially for Rollo May, but choosing it alone leaves out self-determination and Viktor Frankl's theme of meaning.", "c": "Meaning is central to Frankl's logotherapy and to Yalom's concern with meaninglessness, but choosing it alone leaves out freedom and self-determination."}}, {"q": "Which of the following deals with client's rights and responsibilities in context of counseling?", "concept": "The answer is c: informed consent is the part of counselling that deals with the client's rights and responsibilities. Informed consent means the client agrees to counselling freely, after being told in plain language what it involves. Before counselling begins, the counsellor explains the nature, goals and likely length of the work, the methods, the counsellor's qualifications, fees, and the limits of confidentiality (for example, risk of serious harm to self or others, child abuse, or a court order). The client learns his or her rights: to ask questions, to refuse a technique and to end counselling at any time. Responsibilities are spelt out too: attending sessions, paying fees and working towards agreed goals. Informed consent is an ongoing process, revisited as the work changes, not a single signature. It appears in the American Counseling Association Code of Ethics (2014, section A.2) and in the American Psychological Association Ethics Code (Standard 10.01, informed consent to therapy). Example: before her first session, Meera is told that what she says stays private unless someone is in danger, what each session costs, and that she may stop whenever she wishes; only then does she agree. Option c is right because informed consent is the ethical process built around the client's rights and responsibilities. Exam trap: a counselling contract also mentions fees and goals, so students pick 'Contract' or 'All of these'; but a contract records the working arrangement, while rights and responsibilities are the content of informed consent.", "traps": {"a": "A counselling contract is the working agreement on goals, session times, fees and duration, often drawn up during the consent process; it records arrangements but is not the ethical concept that defines the client's rights and responsibilities.", "b": "'Covenant' is a religious or legal word for a solemn promise; it is not a recognised term in counselling ethics, so it is a filler.", "d": "'All of these' fails because covenant is not a counselling term at all and a contract only sets the working arrangements; informed consent alone covers rights and responsibilities."}}, {"q": "In psychoanalytic counseling which of the following methods are used?", "concept": "The answer is c: psychoanalytic counselling uses both free association and dream analysis. Psychoanalysis, the therapy of Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), aims to make unconscious conflicts conscious so that the client can understand and work through them. Its methods are built to get round the mind's censorship. In free association the client, often lying on a couch with the analyst out of sight, follows the fundamental rule: say whatever comes to mind, however trivial, embarrassing or illogical. Freud adopted it in the 1890s after giving up hypnosis, because many patients could not be hypnotised. Where the flow of associations stops or swerves, the analyst sees resistance and a clue to hidden material. In dream analysis the client recalls a dream and free-associates to each part of it. In The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) Freud called the interpretation of dreams 'the royal road' to the unconscious: the remembered manifest content disguises a hidden latent wish through condensation, displacement and symbolisation. The analyst also interprets resistance and transference. Example: Kabir dreams of missing a train; associating to it, he reaches his fear of failing his father's expectations. Option c is right because free association and dream analysis are both core psychoanalytic techniques, so neither (A) nor (B) alone is complete. Exam trap: the interview is used by every school of counselling, from person-centred to behavioural, so it is not a distinctively psychoanalytic method.", "traps": {"a": "Free association, Freud's fundamental rule of saying whatever comes to mind, is a psychoanalytic method, but choosing it alone leaves out dream analysis, which is equally part of the method.", "b": "Dream analysis, Freud's 'royal road' to the unconscious, is psychoanalytic, but choosing it alone leaves out free association, on which dream interpretation itself depends.", "d": "The interview method is a general tool of assessment and counselling used by every approach; it is not a technique specific to psychoanalysis."}}, {"q": "SUDS is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is a: SUDS is associated with systematic desensitisation. SUDS stands for the Subjective Units of Disturbance (or Distress) Scale, a self-rating from 0 (completely calm) to 100 (the worst anxiety imaginable); a 0 to 10 version is also used. Joseph Wolpe, who developed systematic desensitisation (Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, 1958), devised the scale; it is usually cited to his The Practice of Behavior Therapy (1969). In desensitisation the client first learns deep muscle relaxation, then builds an anxiety hierarchy of 10 to 20 feared situations, each given a SUDS rating so they can be ranked from least to most frightening. While relaxed, the client imagines each item in turn and moves up only when its SUDS rating has dropped to a low level. Relaxation, being incompatible with anxiety, gradually replaces the fear: reciprocal inhibition. Example: Riya, afraid of lifts, rates looking at a lift 15, standing inside with the door open 40 and riding ten floors 90, then works up the list. Option a is right because SUDS was created to build and climb the desensitisation hierarchy. Exam trap: SUDS rates anxiety, so students assume every anxiety-related therapy uses it and choose 'All of these'; aversion therapy, which pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus, has no need for an anxiety ladder. Key note: SUDS is also used to track anxiety during flooding and other exposure therapies, so (B) has some basis; but aversion therapy does not use it, so 'All of these' fails, and systematic desensitisation is the classic pairing (IHBAS 2020 keyed the same item this way); a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Flooding (and Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy, 1967) exposes the client at once to the most feared situation until anxiety falls; therapists may track SUDS during it, but the scale was built for the graded hierarchy of desensitisation.", "c": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking, with an unpleasant stimulus like a nausea-inducing drug; it builds discomfort rather than reducing fear, so it has no use for an anxiety hierarchy rated in SUDS.", "d": "'All of these' fails because aversion therapy does not use SUDS; systematic desensitisation is the defining pairing."}}, {"q": "Carl Rogers's theory is based on how many major propositions?", "concept": "The answer is b: Carl Rogers stated his theory of personality as 19 propositions. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), founder of client-centred (later person-centred) therapy, set them out in the chapter 'A Theory of Personality and Behavior' in Client-Centered Therapy (1951). The first proposition says that every individual exists in a continually changing world of experience of which he is the centre: the phenomenal field. Later propositions add that a person reacts to the field as he perceives it, not as it objectively is; that part of the field becomes differentiated as the self; that experiences consistent with the self-concept are accepted, while inconsistent ones are denied or distorted; and that this incongruence between self and experience produces tension, anxiety and defence. Under conditions of safety the person can admit such experiences and move towards becoming a fully functioning person. Example: Arjun sees himself as a calm person, so he denies his anger at his brother; in a safe, accepting therapy he can admit it, and his self-concept widens. Option b is right because the theory is stated in exactly 19 propositions. Exam trap: other Rogerian numbers sit close by: 3 core conditions (congruence, unconditional positive regard and empathic understanding) and 6 necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change (1957). Learn which number goes with which list.", "traps": {"a": "Nine is a filler; the numbers that matter in Rogers's theory are 19 propositions, 6 conditions of therapeutic change and 3 core conditions.", "c": "Three is the number of Rogers's core therapist conditions (congruence, unconditional positive regard and empathy), not the number of propositions in his personality theory.", "d": "Twenty-one is a filler close to 19; Rogers's 1951 statement has 19 propositions, not 21."}}, {"q": "The concept of measurement scales was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the concept of scales (levels) of measurement was given by S. S. Stevens. Stanley Smith Stevens (1906 to 1973), a Harvard psychophysicist, set it out in 'On the Theory of Scales of Measurement' (Science, 1946). He defined measurement as assigning numbers to objects or events according to rules, and argued that different rules create four scales, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names categories (blood group, the number on a cricketer's shirt). An ordinal scale adds order (class rank). An interval scale adds equal units but has an arbitrary zero (Celsius temperature, IQ). A ratio scale adds a true zero, so ratios are meaningful (weight, reaction time). Stevens tied each scale to its permissible statistics, which textbooks summarise as mode and chi-square for nominal data, median and rank correlation for ordinal, mean, SD and Pearson r for interval, and all statistics, including the coefficient of variation, for ratio. Example: in Meera's survey, gender is nominal, rank in class is ordinal, an IQ score is interval and monthly income is ratio. Option a is right because Stevens proposed the four-scale scheme. Exam trap: the other three are famous statisticians whose names appear beside the scales in textbooks (Pearson r for interval data, Spearman's rho for ordinal), but none of them classified the scales.", "traps": {"b": "Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) pioneered the study of individual differences and mental tests and introduced regression (1886) and correlation (1888), but he did not classify scales of measurement.", "c": "Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave the product-moment correlation coefficient (1896) and the chi-square test (1900), statistics used on interval and nominal data, not the scheme of scales itself.", "d": "Charles Spearman (1863 to 1945) introduced rank correlation and the g factor (both 1904); his rho suits ordinal data, but he did not propose the levels of measurement."}}, {"q": "The ratio scale has:", "concept": "The answer is a: the ratio scale has a true zero. A true (absolute) zero means zero amount of the thing measured: 0 kg is no weight, 0 seconds is no time, 0 errors is no errors. Because zero is real, ratios make sense: a reaction time of 600 milliseconds is twice one of 300, and Kabir, who earns 40,000 rupees a month, earns twice as much as Arjun at 20,000. Ratio is the highest of the four levels of measurement set out by Stanley Smith Stevens (1946): it has identity, order, equal intervals and a true zero, so it permits every statistic, including the geometric mean and the coefficient of variation. Examples in psychology are reaction time, number of correct answers, height, weight and income. An interval scale has equal units but an arbitrary zero chosen by convention. On the Celsius scale 0 degrees is the freezing point of water, not the absence of heat, so 20 degrees is not twice as hot as 10 degrees; IQ and calendar years work the same way. Option a is right because the true zero is the defining feature of a ratio scale. Exam trap: the arbitrary zero belongs to the interval scale, one level lower, and examiners swap the two.", "traps": {"b": "An arbitrary zero is the mark of the interval scale (Celsius temperature, IQ, calendar years), where zero is a convention and ratios are not meaningful.", "c": "The ratio scale is the most informative scale and permits all statistical operations, so it has the fewest limitations, not many; the nominal scale is the most limited.", "d": "'None of these' is wrong because option a, the true zero, correctly describes the ratio scale."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an alternative for C-alpha?", "concept": "The answer is b: composite reliability is the standard alternative to Cronbach's alpha. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) estimates internal consistency, how well the items of a scale hang together, from the number of items and how strongly they covary. It assumes that every item measures the construct equally well (tau-equivalence, or equal loadings). When some items are much stronger than others, alpha tends to underestimate the true reliability. Composite reliability, set out by Charles Werts, Robert Linn and Karl Joreskog (1974) and made popular in structural equation modelling by Claes Fornell and David Larcker (1981), uses the factor loadings from a confirmatory factor analysis: it weights each item by how strongly it reflects the construct, then compares true-score variance with total variance. Values of about 0.70 or more are usually taken as acceptable. Roderick McDonald's omega (Test Theory: A Unified Treatment, 1999) is essentially the same index for a one-factor scale. Example: Riya's five-item stress scale has two weak items; alpha comes out at 0.72, but composite reliability, giving the strong items more weight, is 0.76. Option b is right because composite reliability is the reliability index used in place of alpha in factor-based research. Exam trap: Spearman's rho and Pearson's r are correlations between two variables, not measures of a scale's internal consistency. Key note: some structural equation modelling texts (Joseph Hair and colleagues' Multivariate Data Analysis) call the same index 'construct reliability', so option a is close; but 'composite reliability' is the usual name for the alternative to alpha, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Construct reliability is a name some structural equation modelling texts give to the same loading-based index, but the established name for the alternative to alpha is composite reliability.", "c": "Spearman's rho (Charles Spearman, 1904) is a rank-order correlation between two variables for ordinal data; it does not estimate a scale's internal consistency.", "d": "Pearson's product-moment coefficient (Karl Pearson, 1896) measures the linear relation between two interval variables; it is used for test-retest or parallel-forms reliability, not as a replacement for alpha."}}, {"q": "The type of validity which is concerned with the question of whether the results support the theory behind the research is:", "concept": "The answer is b: construct validity asks whether the results support the theory behind the research. A construct is an unobservable theoretical idea, such as anxiety, intelligence or self-esteem. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that a test measures a construct only if its scores behave the way the theory says they should, fitting a nomological network of predicted relations. Evidence comes from several sources. Convergent validity: the test correlates with other measures of the same construct. Discriminant validity: it does not correlate highly with measures of different constructs. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) checked both together in the multitrait-multimethod matrix. Experimental evidence also counts: if theory says anxiety rises before an exam, scores should rise too. Example: Kabir's new test anxiety scale correlates .65 with an established anxiety inventory and only .10 with a vocabulary test, and scores rise on the morning of an exam, as the theory predicts, so its construct validity is supported. Option b is right because construct validity is the only option defined by agreement between the results and the underlying theory. Exam trap: concurrent and predictive validity check a test against a practical criterion, not against theory, and external validity concerns generalisation; examiners rely on students blurring the different kinds of validity.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity is a form of criterion-related validity: test scores are compared with a criterion measured at the same time, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis; it checks agreement with a criterion, not with theory.", "c": "External validity, made standard by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963), is whether a study's findings generalise to other people, settings and times, not whether they fit the theory.", "d": "Face validity is whether a test looks, to test takers, as if it measures what it claims; it is a matter of appearance, not of theoretical support."}}, {"q": "A researcher has developed two equivalent versions of a test and has administered them on the same set of participants on two different occasions. He is using _____ method of reliability.", "concept": "The answer is d: giving two equivalent versions of a test to the same people is the alternate-forms (parallel or equivalent forms) method of reliability. The two forms are built from the same blueprint: same content areas, same number and type of items, same difficulty, and similar means and variances. Both are given to the same group and the two sets of scores are correlated. If they are given in one sitting, the correlation is the coefficient of equivalence, which reflects error from content sampling (which particular items happened to be chosen). When, as in this stem, there is a time gap between the two, the correlation is the coefficient of stability and equivalence, which captures both content error and error from changes over time, so it is usually the strictest estimate. Parallel forms also reduce practice and memory effects, since the second test has new items. Example: Meera builds Form A and Form B of a 40-item arithmetic test, gives Form A to a class in July and Form B in August, and finds r = .84. Option d is right because two equivalent versions given to the same people define the alternate-forms method. Exam trap: the 'two occasions' wording pulls students towards test-retest, but test-retest uses the same form both times; the deciding detail is 'two equivalent versions'.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest gives the same form twice to the same people, and the correlation is the coefficient of stability; here two different equivalent forms were used, so it is not test-retest.", "b": "Split-half gives one test once, splits it into halves (for example odd and even items), correlates them and corrects with the Spearman-Brown formula; no second form or occasion is involved.", "c": "Path analysis, devised by Sewall Wright (1921), is a regression-based method for testing causal models among variables; it is not a method of estimating reliability."}}, {"q": "According to R. Cattell in the 16PF, \"Suspicious vs Trusting\" describes:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the 16PF, 'Suspicious versus Trusting' is factor L, Vigilance. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis of trait ratings, questionnaires and objective tests to find 16 source traits, the basic dimensions underlying personality, and built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, first published 1949; fifth edition 1993) to measure them. Each factor is bipolar and reported as a sten score from 1 to 10. Low scorers on Vigilance are trusting, unsuspecting and accepting, ready to take people at their word. High scorers are vigilant, suspicious, sceptical and wary, expecting to be misled. The other factors are A Warmth, B Reasoning, C Emotional Stability, E Dominance, F Liveliness, G Rule-Consciousness, H Social Boldness, I Sensitivity, M Abstractedness, N Privateness, O Apprehension, Q1 Openness to Change, Q2 Self-Reliance, Q3 Perfectionism and Q4 Tension. Example: Arjun double-checks every bill and assumes the shopkeeper is cheating him; he would score high on factor L. Option a is right because Vigilance is the factor whose poles are trusting and suspicious. Exam trap: the factor letters are not consecutive (there is no D, J or K), and the label 'Vigilance' does not obviously suggest trust, so students mismatch it with Dominance or Self-Reliance.", "traps": {"b": "Self-Reliance is factor Q2, running from group-oriented (prefers to work with others) to self-reliant (solitary, prefers own decisions); it is about independence, not trust.", "c": "Dominance is factor E, from deferential, cooperative and conflict-avoiding to dominant, forceful and assertive; it concerns control over others, not suspicion.", "d": "Liveliness is factor F, from serious, restrained and careful to lively, spontaneous and enthusiastic; it is about expressiveness, not trust."}}, {"q": "Murray is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: Henry Murray is associated with both the TAT and Herman Melville studies. Henry Murray (1893 to 1988), of the Harvard Psychological Clinic, created the Thematic Apperception Test with Christiana Morgan (1935). The person tells a story about each of a series of ambiguous pictures (31 cards in the standard set, one of them blank), saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. The stories are read for the hero's needs and for the press (environmental forces) acting on the hero, following Murray's theory of personology in Explorations in Personality (1938), with needs such as achievement, affiliation and dominance. Murray was also a lifelong scholar of the novelist Herman Melville (1819 to 1891), whose work he read as a study in unconscious conflict. He edited Melville's novel Pierre (1949) with a long psychological introduction and wrote the essay 'In Nomine Diaboli' (1951) on Moby-Dick. Example: Kabir, shown a boy gazing at a violin, tells of a child forced to practise who dreams of escape, hinting at a need for autonomy. Option c is right because both statements describe Murray's work, so neither (A) nor (B) alone is complete. Exam trap: the Rorschach is the other famous projective test, so it sits beside the TAT in options, but it belongs to Hermann Rorschach, not to Murray.", "traps": {"a": "The TAT (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) is Murray's best-known work, but choosing it alone leaves out his decades of Melville scholarship.", "b": "Murray did study Herman Melville (his edition of Pierre, 1949, and the essay on Moby-Dick, 1951), but choosing this alone leaves out the TAT, his main contribution to assessment.", "d": "The Rorschach inkblot test was published by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in Psychodiagnostik (1921); it is unrelated to Murray."}}, {"q": "Rorschach is the author of which of the following book/s?", "concept": "The answer is a: Hermann Rorschach wrote Psychodiagnostik. Hermann Rorschach (1884 to 1922), a Swiss psychiatrist, published Psychodiagnostik in 1921, the monograph that introduced his inkblot method. He tried many blots on patients and healthy people and settled on 15, but the publisher would print only ten, so the test has ten cards. The ten bilaterally symmetrical cards are always shown in the same order: five achromatic, black and grey (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two black and red (II, III) and three multicoloured (VIII, IX, X). The person says what each blot might be, and responses are scored for location (whole blot or a detail), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading) and content. Rorschach died the year after the book appeared, aged 37, so others developed the scoring, most influentially John Exner with his Comprehensive System (1974). Example: Meera sees a bat in Card V and two dancers in Card II; the scorer codes where in the blot she looked and what made it look that way. Option a is right because Psychodiagnostik is the only real book among the options. Exam trap: 'Inkblot Test' is the name of his test, not the title of a book, and the language of 'projection' and 'projective methods' came from other people.", "traps": {"b": "'Inkblot Test' is the popular name of the Rorschach technique itself, the test described in Psychodiagnostik, not a book Rorschach wrote.", "c": "Projection as a defence comes from Sigmund Freud (1890s), and Lawrence Frank (1939) coined 'projective methods'; Rorschach wrote no book called Projection Theory.", "d": "'All of these' fails because only Psychodiagnostik is a book by Rorschach; the other two options are the name of a test and a concept."}}, {"q": "The qualitative and quantitative research paradigms are:", "concept": "The answer is a: qualitative and quantitative research paradigms are complementary to each other. Quantitative research measures variables with numbers, uses larger and often probability samples, tests hypotheses statistically and aims at prediction and generalisation (experiments, surveys). Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and context through words and observation, uses small purposive samples and interprets data inductively (interviews, ethnography, grounded theory, case studies). Each answers questions the other cannot: numbers show how much and how often, words show how and why. Combining the two in one study is mixed methods research, set out by John Creswell and Vicki Plano Clark (2007): for example, a survey followed by interviews that explain its findings. Using several methods to cross-check a conclusion is triangulation (Norman Denzin, 1978); when both kinds of evidence agree, confidence in the finding rises. Example: Arjun surveys 300 college students and finds that 40 per cent feel lonely, then interviews 12 of them to learn what loneliness feels like and what makes it worse; together the two give a fuller picture than either alone. Option a is right because the two approaches answer different parts of a question and are routinely combined. Exam trap: in the 'paradigm wars' of the 1980s some writers argued that the two were incompatible, which makes 'contradictory' sound plausible; the modern consensus is that they complement each other.", "traps": {"b": "'Contradictory' reflects the old paradigm wars view that the two rest on incompatible philosophies; mixed methods research shows they can be used together, so they are not contradictory.", "c": "Both are scientific: they are systematic, empirical and open to scrutiny, and qualitative research has its own quality standards, such as credibility and trustworthiness (Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba, 1985).", "d": "Both are used across the sciences, including medicine, nursing and psychology; 'direct sciences' is not a recognised category, and neither approach is ruled out of empirical work."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a step of research?", "concept": "The answer is b: data manipulating is not a step of research. A research study follows an ordered series of steps. The researcher identifies and defines a problem, reviews the literature, frames hypotheses, chooses a design and a sample, collects data, analyses them and tests the hypotheses, interprets and discusses the findings, and writes the report, which often raises the next problem. C. R. Kothari's Research Methodology lays out this same flow. Data manipulation, in the sense of altering, selecting or inventing data so that results come out as hoped, is not a step but research misconduct. Misconduct is usually defined as fabrication (making up data), falsification (changing data, procedures or results so that the record misrepresents the research) and plagiarism, often shortened to FFP. Example: Riya finds that her mindfulness group did no better than the controls; quietly dropping the three participants who spoiled her result to report p < .05 would be falsification, whereas reporting the null result honestly is the discussion step. Option b is right because manipulating data corrupts research rather than forming part of it. Exam trap: researchers do manipulate the independent variable in an experiment, which is legitimate design; the item is about manipulating data, which is not. Key note: in statistics software 'data manipulation' can also mean routine cleaning, recoding and transforming of data, which is part of analysis; read here as falsifying data, it is not a step, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Hypothesis testing is a core step: after data are collected and analysed, statistical tests decide whether the results support the hypotheses.", "c": "Discussion of findings is a core step: the researcher interprets the results, relates them to earlier studies, notes limitations and draws conclusions.", "d": "'None of these' would mean all three are steps, but data manipulating is not a legitimate step, so option b is the answer instead."}}, {"q": "Which law deals with the sample selection?", "concept": "The answer is a: the law of large numbers deals with sample selection. Sampling theory, as taught in Indian statistics and research methods texts, rests on two laws. The law of statistical regularity says that a reasonably large sample drawn at random from a population will, on average, have the characteristics of that population. The law of inertia of large numbers says that large groups are more stable than small ones: in a big sample, unusual cases in one direction tend to be balanced by those in the other, so errors cancel out. Both are informal versions of the law of large numbers, first proved (for proportions) by Jacob Bernoulli in Ars Conjectandi (published 1713): as a random sample grows, its mean or proportion gets closer and closer to the population value. This is why researchers prefer larger random samples. Example: if Kabir picks 5 students at random from a college of 2,000, their average height may be far off; with 200 students it will be very close to the true college average. Option a is right because the law of large numbers justifies drawing conclusions about a population from a random sample. Exam trap: the 'law of small numbers' is not a sampling law but a bias, described by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1971), of wrongly expecting small samples to mirror the population. Key note: the laws usually named in research methods texts are the law of statistical regularity and the law of inertia of large numbers; of the options only the law of large numbers covers them, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The 'law of small numbers' is Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman's (1971) ironic name for a cognitive bias, the belief that small samples resemble the population; it warns against small samples rather than guiding selection.", "c": "'Law of representative numbers' is not a law; it echoes the idea of a representative sample and the representativeness heuristic but is a filler.", "d": "'None of these' is wrong because the law of large numbers, option a, is the law on which sample selection rests."}}, {"q": "Which of the following violates the ethical principles of research?", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these violates the ethical principles of research. All three are standard steps in data preparation, the stage between collecting data and analysing them, as described in Naresh Malhotra's Marketing Research. Neutralising missing data means treating missing responses, for example by substituting a neutral value, usually the mean of that variable, so the case can still be used; the alternatives are imputing a value from the person's other answers, casewise deletion and pairwise deletion. Standardising data is a scale transformation, such as converting raw scores to z scores (mean 0, SD 1) so that variables measured in different units can be compared or combined. Removing outliers means screening for extreme values, such as an age typed as 210, and deleting or adjusting them for a stated reason. Each is ethical when done by a rule decided in advance and reported openly. Research ethics is violated by fabrication, falsification and plagiarism, and by harm to participants, deception without debriefing, breach of confidentiality or the lack of informed consent. Example: Meera replaces four missing items with the item mean, converts scores to z scores and drops one impossible reaction time of 0.01 seconds, noting each step in her method section. Option d is right because each listed step is a legitimate, reportable part of data preparation. Exam trap: words like 'removing' and 'neutralising' sound like tampering, but open data cleaning is not misconduct; hidden or selective handling is. Key note: removing outliers selectively just to reach significance would be falsification, but as stated each is a legitimate step, so 'None of these' stands.", "traps": {"a": "Neutralising missing data, for example substituting the variable's mean for a missing response, is a recognised treatment of missing values in data preparation, not an ethical breach.", "b": "Standardising data, such as converting scores to z scores, is a legitimate scale transformation that puts variables on a common metric; it does not distort the findings.", "c": "Removing outliers is legitimate data cleaning when done by a stated rule and reported; it becomes falsification only if done selectively to get a desired result."}}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of grade correlation?", "concept": "The answer is a: Charles Spearman gave the concept of grade correlation. Charles Spearman (1863 to 1945), the British psychologist also known for the g factor, introduced rank correlation in 'The proof and measurement of association between two things' (American Journal of Psychology, 1904). Instead of using raw scores, each person is ranked on both variables and the differences between the two ranks (D) are used: rho = 1 minus 6 times the sum of D squared, divided by N(N squared minus 1). When the ranks are expressed as grades, that is, percentile positions within the group, the same coefficient is called grade correlation, and the name, though now rare, is still in use. It suits ordinal data, such as class ranks or judges' ratings, and relations that rise or fall steadily without being straight lines, and it is less affected by outliers than Pearson's r. Example: two judges rank ten Bharatanatyam dancers; Arjun works out the rank differences and gets rho = .85, showing strong agreement. Option a is right because grade (rank) correlation comes from Spearman. Exam trap: Karl Pearson is the name most linked with correlation, so students pick him, but his product-moment r (1896) uses actual scores, not ranks or grades. Key note: Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) had earlier worked with 'grades' (percentile ranks), and Pearson later wrote on correlation from grades; but textbooks credit grade (rank) correlation to Spearman, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave the product-moment correlation coefficient r (1896), computed from actual interval scores rather than ranks or grades.", "c": "'Adams' is a filler; no theorist of that name is linked with grade or rank correlation.", "d": "Francis Galton (1822 to 1911) introduced the idea of correlation (1888) and used percentile 'grades' to describe ability, but the grade (rank) correlation coefficient is credited to Spearman."}}, {"q": "A negative correlation between X and Y can be referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is d: a negative correlation can be called both an anticorrelation and an inverse correlation. A correlation describes how two variables move together. In a negative correlation, high scores on X go with low scores on Y: as one rises, the other falls. The coefficient r lies between 0 and minus 1, and the scatterplot slopes downwards from left to right. Minus 1 is a perfect negative relation; values near 0 mean little linear relation. The sign shows direction only, and strength is shown by size, so r = minus .80 is stronger than r = plus .30. Inverse correlation is the everyday textbook synonym: the two variables are inversely related. Anticorrelation is the same idea, used especially in physics and brain imaging; Michael Fox and colleagues (2005) showed that activity in the brain's default mode network is anticorrelated with task-positive attention networks, falling when they rise. Example: the more hours Riya spends on her phone at night, the fewer hours she sleeps; phone use and sleep are negatively, or inversely, correlated. Option d is right because both (A) and (B) are accepted names for a negative correlation, so neither alone is complete. Exam trap: 'false correlation' sounds like a technical term, but the real near-miss is spurious correlation, a term Karl Pearson (1897) coined for misleading correlations between ratios, now used for any correlation produced by a third variable or by chance, which can be positive or negative.", "traps": {"a": "Anticorrelation is a correct name for a negative correlation, used especially in physics and brain imaging, but choosing it alone leaves out inverse correlation, which is equally correct.", "b": "Inverse correlation is a correct name for a negative correlation, but choosing it alone leaves out anticorrelation, so the combined option is the complete answer.", "c": "'False correlation' is not a statistical term; the near-miss is spurious correlation (a term from Karl Pearson, 1897), now meaning an apparent relation caused by a third variable or by chance, which says nothing about direction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not associated with the t-test?", "concept": "The answer is d: mean distortion is not associated with the t-test; it is not a statistical term at all. The t-test, published by William Sealy Gosset under the pen name 'Student' (1908) while he worked at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, compares means: one sample against a known value, two independent groups, or the same people measured twice. The other three options are all part of using it. The researcher computes t, the difference between means divided by its standard error. This value is compared with the critical value from the t table for the chosen alpha level (usually .05) and the degrees of freedom; if t exceeds it, the null hypothesis is rejected. Software reports the p value, the probability of a difference at least this large if the null hypothesis were true. Because a significant t says nothing about size, the effect size is reported too, usually Cohen's d, the mean difference in SD units, with Jacob Cohen's (1988) benchmarks of 0.2 small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large. Example: Kabir finds that students who revise with flashcards outscore others, t(58) = 2.40, p = .02, d = 0.63, a medium to large effect. Option d is right because mean distortion is an invented phrase with no place in t-test procedure. Exam trap: examiners place one made-up term among real ones; check each option against what you know rather than picking the one that sounds least familiar or most technical.", "traps": {"a": "The p value is reported with every t-test: the probability of a difference at least as large as the one found if the null hypothesis were true.", "b": "The critical value of t, read from the t table for the chosen alpha and degrees of freedom, is the cut-off the computed t must exceed for significance.", "c": "Effect size, usually Cohen's d (Jacob Cohen, 1988), is reported alongside a t-test to show how large the difference between the means is."}}, {"q": "Which of the following tests is related with the goodness of fit concept?", "concept": "The answer is b: the chi-square test is the test related to goodness of fit. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for frequency data. A goodness of fit test asks whether the observed frequencies in the categories of one variable match the frequencies expected under some hypothesis. The statistic is chi-square = the sum, over all categories, of (O minus E) squared divided by E, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of categories minus 1. A small chi-square means a good fit; a large one means the data depart from the expected pattern. A classic use is testing the 3:1 ratio of dominant to recessive pea plants predicted by Gregor Mendel (1866). Chi-square also serves as a test of independence for two categorical variables in a contingency table. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four tea brands they prefer; equal preference predicts 30 per brand, and her observed counts of 45, 30, 25 and 20 give chi-square = 11.67 with 3 degrees of freedom, above the .05 critical value of 7.81, so preferences are not equal. Option b is right because chi-square goodness of fit is the standard test comparing observed with expected frequencies. Exam trap: the CMH and McNemar tests also produce chi-square statistics, so 'All of these' tempts, but neither tests the fit of one distribution to a hypothesised one.", "traps": {"a": "The Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel (CMH) test (William Cochran, 1954; Nathan Mantel and William Haenszel, 1959) tests the association between two binary variables across several strata of 2 x 2 tables; it is a test of association, not goodness of fit.", "c": "The McNemar test (Quinn McNemar, 1947) tests change in paired nominal data, such as the same people's yes or no answers before and after an intervention; it is not a goodness of fit test.", "d": "'All of these' fails because only chi-square tests the fit of observed to expected frequencies; CMH and McNemar test association and change."}}]});
